/* Ohmly course content. Original text. Module fields: n, part (A foundations, B building, C deeper), title, short, blurb, big, ideas, formulas, tip, labs, quiz, reading, ex (worked examples: q, steps, ans), deep (optional depth: t, body; body blocks split by a blank line, "= " lines are formulas, "- " lines are lists) */
(function(){
const M = [
{"n":1,"title":"Voltage, Current and Resistance","short":"V · I · R","blurb":"The three quantities behind every circuit, and the law that ties them together.","big":"Every circuit you will ever build comes down to three quantities. Voltage is the push, current is the flow, and resistance is what limits the flow. Ohm's law links them, and the power formula tells you how much heat a part has to get rid of. Learn these until they are automatic and the rest of electronics gets much easier.","ideas":["Current (I, in amps) is the rate at which charge flows past a point. One amp is about 6.2 × 10¹⁸ electrons per second.","Voltage (V, in volts) is the energy each unit of charge gains or loses between two points. It is always measured between two points, never at one.","Resistance (R, in ohms, Ω) is how strongly a part opposes current.","Ohm's law, V = I × R, lets you find any one of the three from the other two.","Power is P = V × I. Every part has a power rating, and exceeding it makes the part overheat.","Conventional current is drawn flowing from + to −. Electrons actually drift the other way, but every schematic and textbook uses the conventional direction.","A short circuit has almost zero resistance, so the current is limited only by the source. An open circuit has no path, so no current flows."],"formulas":[["V = I · R","Ohm's law"],["P = V · I","Power"],["P = I² · R","Power from current and resistance"],["P = V² / R","Power from voltage and resistance"]],"tip":"When a circuit doesn't work, write down V, I and R for each part. Most mistakes become obvious once you put numbers to them.","labs":["ohm"],"quiz":[{"q":"A 9 V battery drives a 470 Ω resistor. What current flows?","o":["0.52 mA","19 mA","4.2 A","52 mA"],"a":1,"w":"I = V / R = 9 / 470 ≈ 0.019 A, which is 19 mA."},{"q":"How much power does that resistor dissipate?","o":["0.17 W","1.7 W","0.017 W","4.2 W"],"a":0,"w":"P = V² / R = 81 / 470 ≈ 0.17 W. A standard ¼ W resistor copes, but it runs warm."},{"q":"A resistor carries 20 mA and has 4 V across it. Its resistance is:","o":["80 Ω","200 Ω","5 Ω","0.08 Ω"],"a":1,"w":"R = V / I = 4 / 0.02 = 200 Ω."},{"q":"You double the voltage across a fixed resistor. The power it dissipates:","o":["Stays the same","Doubles","Quadruples","Halves"],"a":2,"w":"P = V² / R, so twice the voltage gives four times the power."},{"q":"Which statement about voltage is true?","o":["It flows through a wire","It is measured between two points","It is measured in amps","It is zero in a short circuit everywhere"],"a":1,"w":"Voltage is a difference in energy per charge between two points. Current is the thing that flows."}],"reading":"pp. 5–73","part":"A","ex":[{"q":"A 9 V battery feeds a 330 Ω resistor. Find the current and the power, and decide whether a ¼ W resistor will do.","steps":["Current: I = V / R = 9 V / 330 Ω = 27.3 mA.","Power: P = V · I = 9 V × 27.3 mA = 245 mW. Check with P = V² / R = 81 V² / 330 Ω = 245 mW.","A ¼ W part is rated 250 mW, so this one would run at 98% of its rating and get very hot.","Pick the next standard rating, ½ W (500 mW). That leaves about 2× headroom."],"ans":"I = 27.3 mA, P = 245 mW. Use a ½ W resistor."},{"q":"What is the largest current a 100 Ω, ½ W resistor can carry continuously, and what voltage is across it then?","steps":["Start from P = I² · R, so I = √(P / R).","I = √(0.5 W / 100 Ω) = √(0.005 A²) = 70.7 mA.","Voltage: V = I · R = 70.7 mA × 100 Ω = 7.07 V.","Check: V² / R = (7.07 V)² / 100 Ω = 50 V² / 100 Ω = 0.5 W."],"ans":"70.7 mA, at 7.07 V."}],"deep":[{"t":"Resistivity and wire gauge","body":"A wire is a long, thin resistor. Its resistance depends on the material, the length and the cross-section:\n\n= R = ρ · L / A\n\nρ (rho) is the resistivity. For copper at 20 °C it is about 1.72 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m. Thicker wire means larger A and less resistance.\n\n22 AWG wire is 0.644 mm across, so A = π × (0.322 mm)² = 0.326 mm².\n\n= R/L = 1.72 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m / 3.26 × 10⁻⁷ m² = 0.053 Ω/m\n\nExample: a lamp 5 m from its supply, so 10 m of wire out and back, carrying 1 A:\n\n- R = 10 m × 0.053 Ω/m = 0.53 Ω\n- Drop = 1 A × 0.53 Ω = 0.53 V, which is 10.6% of a 5 V supply\n- Heat in the wire = I² · R = 0.53 W\n\nAWG numbers run backwards: a higher number is thinner wire. Each 3 steps roughly halves the area, so it doubles the resistance. Where the simple model breaks: at high frequency, current crowds toward the surface of the wire (skin effect) and the effective area shrinks."},{"t":"Temperature coefficient","body":"Metals get more resistive as they warm up, almost linearly:\n\n= R(T) = R₂₀ · [1 + α · (T − 20 °C)]\n\nFor copper, α ≈ 0.0039 per °C.\n\nExample: a copper coil measures 10.0 Ω at 20 °C. After running, it is at 80 °C:\n\n= R = 10.0 Ω × [1 + 0.0039 × 60] = 12.3 Ω\n\nThat is 23% higher. Measuring a winding's resistance before and after is a standard way to find its temperature without a probe inside.\n\nResistors are built to resist this. A metal-film resistor with 100 ppm/°C changes only 100 × 10⁻⁶ × 60 = 0.6% over the same 60 °C swing. Thermistors go the other way on purpose: an NTC type loses resistance as it heats, by several percent per °C.\n\nWhere the simple model breaks: α itself varies with temperature, and Ohm's law quietly stops being constant when the part is hot enough to change its own resistance. A light bulb's cold resistance is about a tenth of its hot value."},{"t":"Power derating with ambient temperature","body":"A resistor's power rating holds only up to a stated ambient temperature, often 70 °C. Above it the allowed power falls in a straight line to zero at the part's maximum temperature, often 155 °C for metal film.\n\n= P(T) = P_rated · (155 − T) / (155 − 70), for 70 °C ≤ T ≤ 155 °C\n\nExample: a ¼ W resistor in a 100 °C enclosure:\n\n= P = 0.25 W × (155 − 100) / 85 = 0.25 W × 0.647 = 162 mW\n\nThe two temperatures vary by series, so read the derating curve in the datasheet. Practical consequences:\n\n- A part rated 250 mW is already down to 65% in a warm box.\n- Parts packed together heat each other, so derate further.\n- Rule of thumb: run at half the rating or less."}]},
{"n":2,"title":"Circuit Networks","short":"Networks","blurb":"Series and parallel, voltage dividers, Kirchhoff's laws and Thevenin equivalents.","big":"Real circuits combine many parts. Series and parallel rules let you simplify them. Kirchhoff's two laws let you analyze anything, however tangled. The voltage divider is the most common small circuit in electronics, and the Thevenin equivalent explains why a divider sags when you connect a load to it.","ideas":["In series, the same current flows through every part and the resistances add.","In parallel, every part has the same voltage across it. The combined resistance is always smaller than the smallest branch.","Kirchhoff's current law: the total current flowing into a node equals the total flowing out.","Kirchhoff's voltage law: going around any closed loop, the voltage rises and drops add up to zero.","A voltage divider produces a fraction of its input set by the ratio R2 / (R1 + R2).","Any network of sources and resistors, seen from two terminals, behaves like one voltage source with one series resistor. This is its Thevenin equivalent.","A load only gets the full Thevenin voltage if its resistance is much larger than the Thevenin resistance. Aim for at least 10× larger."],"formulas":[["R = R₁ + R₂ + …","Series resistance"],["1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + …","Parallel resistance"],["R = R₁R₂ / (R₁ + R₂)","Two resistors in parallel"],["Vout = Vin · R₂ / (R₁ + R₂)","Voltage divider (no load)"],["Rth = R₁ || R₂","Thevenin resistance of a divider"]],"tip":"Dividers are for setting reference voltages and reading sensors, not for powering things. If you need to power something, use a regulator.","labs":["network","divider"],"quiz":[{"q":"Two 1 kΩ resistors in parallel have a combined resistance of:","o":["2 kΩ","1 kΩ","500 Ω","250 Ω"],"a":2,"w":"n equal resistors in parallel give R / n, so 1000 / 2 = 500 Ω."},{"q":"A 100 Ω and a 300 Ω resistor are in series across 12 V. The voltage across the 300 Ω resistor is:","o":["3 V","9 V","4 V","12 V"],"a":1,"w":"The current is 12 / 400 = 30 mA, and 30 mA × 300 Ω = 9 V. Or use the divider ratio 300/400 × 12."},{"q":"Three wires meet at a node. 2 A flows in on one and 0.5 A in on another. The third wire carries:","o":["1.5 A out","2.5 A out","2.5 A in","0 A"],"a":1,"w":"By Kirchhoff's current law, 2.5 A flows in, so 2.5 A must flow out."},{"q":"You load a divider made of two 10 kΩ resistors on 5 V with a 10 kΩ load. The output:","o":["Stays at 2.5 V","Drops to about 1.67 V","Rises to 3.3 V","Becomes 0 V"],"a":1,"w":"The load in parallel with R2 makes 5 kΩ. Vout = 5 × 5k / (10k + 5k) ≈ 1.67 V."},{"q":"The Thevenin resistance of that divider (two 10 kΩ resistors) is:","o":["20 kΩ","10 kΩ","5 kΩ","2.5 kΩ"],"a":2,"w":"With the source replaced by a short, the two resistors appear in parallel: 10k || 10k = 5 kΩ."},{"q":"Adding another resistor in parallel to a circuit always makes the total resistance:","o":["Larger","Smaller","Unchanged","Equal to the new resistor"],"a":1,"w":"Each parallel branch is another path for current, so the total resistance goes down."}],"reading":"pp. 50–80","part":"A","ex":[{"q":"A divider with R₁ = 22 kΩ on top and R₂ = 10 kΩ to ground is fed from 5 V. Find the output with no load, then with a 47 kΩ load connected from the output to ground.","steps":["Unloaded: Vout = 5 V × 10 kΩ / (22 kΩ + 10 kΩ) = 5 V × 0.3125 = 1.563 V.","The load is in parallel with R₂: 10 kΩ ‖ 47 kΩ = (10 kΩ × 47 kΩ) / 57 kΩ = 8.246 kΩ.","Loaded: Vout = 5 V × 8.246 kΩ / (22 kΩ + 8.246 kΩ) = 5 V × 0.2726 = 1.363 V.","The load pulls the output down by (1.563 V − 1.363 V) / 1.563 V = 12.8%."],"ans":"1.563 V unloaded, 1.363 V loaded (12.8% lower)."},{"q":"A 12 V source feeds R₁ = 1 kΩ in series with R₂ = 2.2 kΩ ‖ R₃ = 3.3 kΩ. Find the source current, the voltage across the parallel pair and each branch current.","steps":["Parallel pair: R₂ ‖ R₃ = (2.2 kΩ × 3.3 kΩ) / (2.2 kΩ + 3.3 kΩ) = 7.26 kΩ² / 5.5 kΩ = 1.32 kΩ.","Total: R = 1 kΩ + 1.32 kΩ = 2.32 kΩ. Source current: I = 12 V / 2.32 kΩ = 5.17 mA.","Voltage across the pair: V = 5.17 mA × 1.32 kΩ = 6.83 V. (R₁ drops the other 5.17 V; 5.17 V + 6.83 V = 12 V.)","Branches: 6.83 V / 2.2 kΩ = 3.10 mA and 6.83 V / 3.3 kΩ = 2.07 mA. Check: 3.10 mA + 2.07 mA = 5.17 mA, as Kirchhoff's current law requires."],"ans":"I = 5.17 mA, V = 6.83 V across the pair, branches 3.10 mA and 2.07 mA."}],"deep":[{"t":"Thevenin and Norton equivalents","body":"Seen from two terminals, any linear network of sources and resistors acts like one voltage source in series with one resistor (Thevenin), or one current source in parallel with one resistor (Norton).\n\n= Vth = open-circuit voltage between the terminals\n= Rth = Vth / Isc, with Isc the short-circuit current\n= In = Vth / Rth\n\nThe quick way to find Rth: switch every voltage source off (replace it with a wire) and calculate the resistance seen from the terminals.\n\nExample: 12 V, R₁ = 3.3 kΩ on top, R₂ = 6.8 kΩ to ground, output between the middle and ground.\n\n- Vth = 12 V × 6.8 kΩ / 10.1 kΩ = 8.08 V\n- Rth = 3.3 kΩ ‖ 6.8 kΩ = 2.22 kΩ\n- Check: shorting the output gives Isc = 12 V / 3.3 kΩ = 3.64 mA, and Vth / Rth = 8.08 V / 2.22 kΩ = 3.64 mA\n\nA load takes the most power when R_L = Rth:\n\n= P_max = Vth² / (4 · Rth) = (8.08 V)² / (4 × 2.22 kΩ) = 7.35 mW\n\nAt that match, half the power is lost in Rth, so efficiency is only 50%. Power systems avoid matching for this reason. Signal systems, where you want the most signal and the loss does not matter, often match.\n\nWhere the model breaks: it only holds for linear parts. A diode or transistor in the network has no single Thevenin equivalent, only one valid near an operating point."},{"t":"Superposition","body":"In a network built only from linear parts (resistors, ideal sources), the response to several sources is the sum of the responses to each source acting alone.\n\nRecipe: switch off every source but one (a voltage source becomes a wire, a current source becomes an open circuit), solve, then repeat for each source and add.\n\nExample: V₁ = 10 V through R₁ = 1 kΩ and V₂ = 5 V through R₂ = 2 kΩ both feed one node, which has R₃ = 2 kΩ to ground.\n\n- V₁ alone: R₂ ‖ R₃ = 1 kΩ, so the node is at 10 V × 1 / (1 + 1) = 5.00 V\n- V₂ alone: R₁ ‖ R₃ = 0.667 kΩ, so the node is at 5 V × 0.667 / (2 + 0.667) = 1.25 V\n- Together: 5.00 V + 1.25 V = 6.25 V\n\nCheck with one equation on the node: (10 − V)/1 k + (5 − V)/2 k = V/2 k gives 4V = 25 V, so V = 6.25 V.\n\nSuperposition does not work for power, because P = V² / R is not linear. Power in R₃ is (6.25 V)² / 2 kΩ = 19.5 mW, but adding the separate results gives only 12.5 mW + 0.78 mW = 13.3 mW."},{"t":"Loading a divider, and how much is too much","body":"With a load R_L on the output, the divider becomes a Thevenin source with Rth = R₁ ‖ R₂ driving R_L. The output relative to the unloaded value is:\n\n= Vloaded / Vth = R_L / (Rth + R_L)\n\nSo the sag depends on the ratio R_L / Rth:\n\n- R_L = Rth: output falls to 50%\n- R_L = 10 × Rth: falls to 91%, a 9.1% error\n- R_L = 100 × Rth: falls to 99%, a 1% error\n\nThe rule \"load at least 10× larger\" therefore still leaves about 9% error. It is fine for biasing, not for measuring. For a 1% result, make the load 100× the Thevenin resistance, or buffer the divider with an op amp.\n\nThe trade-off: lowering R₁ and R₂ cuts the sag but wastes current. A 5 V divider of 2 × 10 kΩ burns 0.25 mA all the time, which matters on a battery."}]},
{"n":3,"title":"Your Workbench","short":"Workbench","blurb":"Safety, breadboards, soldering, the multimeter and the oscilloscope.","big":"Theory turns into working hardware at the bench. This module covers staying safe, prototyping on a breadboard, making joints that last, and the two instruments you will use most. A multimeter tells you numbers. An oscilloscope shows you what a signal does over time, which is where most real problems hide.","ideas":["Mains voltage can kill. Stick to low-voltage DC projects powered by a certified adapter or a bench supply until you have proper training.","Large capacitors in power supplies can stay charged after the device is unplugged.","Breadboards are for quick experiments. Move anything that has to last onto perfboard or a printed circuit board.","Measure voltage across a part (in parallel). Measure current through it (in series), with the red lead moved to the current jack.","Measure resistance only with the power off. Other parts connected in parallel will distort the reading.","A bench supply with an adjustable current limit protects your circuit. Set the limit low and raise it once things work.","An oscilloscope's two key knobs are volts per division (vertical) and time per division (horizontal). The trigger holds a repeating wave still on the screen.","A good solder joint is shiny and shaped like a small volcano. A dull, balled-up joint is usually a cold joint."],"formulas":[["f = 1 / T","Frequency from the period you measure"],["Vpp = divisions × V/div","Peak-to-peak from the scope grid"],["T = divisions × time/div","Period from the scope grid"]],"tip":"Most multimeter accidents happen when the red lead is left in the current jack and you then measure a voltage. That puts a near short across the circuit. Move the lead back every single time.","labs":["scope"],"quiz":[{"q":"To measure the current through an LED, the multimeter goes:","o":["In parallel with the LED","In series with the LED","Across the supply","Anywhere, it doesn't matter"],"a":1,"w":"Current has to pass through the meter, so it goes in series. Move the red lead to the mA or A jack."},{"q":"You should measure a resistor's value in a circuit:","o":["With the power on","With the power off, ideally with one leg lifted","Only with an oscilloscope","With the meter in current mode"],"a":1,"w":"The meter injects its own small current. External power ruins the reading, and parallel parts distort it."},{"q":"A square wave on the scope spans 4 divisions vertically at 0.5 V/div. Its peak-to-peak voltage is:","o":["0.125 V","2 V","4.5 V","8 V"],"a":1,"w":"4 divisions × 0.5 V per division = 2 V."},{"q":"One full cycle spans 5 divisions at 200 µs/div. The frequency is:","o":["1 kHz","5 kHz","200 Hz","40 kHz"],"a":0,"w":"The period is 5 × 200 µs = 1 ms, and 1 / 1 ms = 1 kHz."},{"q":"The trace on your scope keeps sliding sideways. The most likely fix is to adjust:","o":["Volts per division","The trigger level so it crosses the signal","The probe tip","The brightness"],"a":1,"w":"If the trigger level is outside the signal's range, the scope can't lock onto the same point each sweep."},{"q":"What does the current limit on a bench power supply do?","o":["It raises the voltage","It caps how much current can flow, which protects against shorts","It filters noise","It converts AC to DC"],"a":1,"w":"If something draws too much, the supply lowers its voltage to hold the current at the limit you set."}],"reading":"pp. 551–634","part":"A","ex":[{"q":"A scope set to 0.5 V/div and 250 µs/div shows a square wave 3.4 divisions tall and 4.0 divisions per cycle. Find its peak-to-peak voltage, peak value and frequency.","steps":["Peak-to-peak: Vpp = 3.4 div × 0.5 V/div = 1.7 V.","If the wave is centred on 0 V, the peak is 1.7 V / 2 = 0.85 V.","Period: T = 4.0 div × 250 µs/div = 1000 µs = 1.0 ms.","Frequency: f = 1 / T = 1 / 1.0 ms = 1000 Hz = 1 kHz."],"ans":"1.7 V peak-to-peak, 0.85 V peak, 1 kHz."},{"q":"Your meter has no spare current range. A load runs from 5 V, and you put a 1.0 Ω resistor in series and read 38 mV across it. What is the load current, and what does the trick cost?","steps":["Ohm's law on the 1.0 Ω resistor: I = V / R = 38 mV / 1.0 Ω = 38 mA.","Voltage lost from the load's supply: 38 mV, which is 38 mV / 5 V = 0.76%.","Power in the sense resistor: P = V · I = 38 mV × 38 mA = 1.44 mW. A ¼ W part is far more than enough.","A larger resistor gives a bigger, easier reading but steals more voltage. Choose the smallest value that still gives a readable voltage."],"ans":"38 mA, with 0.76% of the supply lost."}],"deep":[{"t":"Meter burden voltage","body":"A multimeter measures current by passing it through a small internal shunt resistor (plus a fuse and the leads) and reading the voltage across it. That voltage is the burden, and the meter is a resistor you have added to your circuit.\n\nExample: a 3.3 V circuit with a 33 Ω load. The true current is 100 mA. A meter range with 2 Ω of total burden resistance changes it:\n\n= I = 3.3 V / (33 Ω + 2 Ω) = 94.3 mA\n\nThat is 5.7% low, and 0.19 V of the 3.3 V is dropped inside the meter. Actual burden varies with the range: the microamp ranges use shunts of hundreds of ohms, the amp range a fraction of an ohm. Read the specification for the range you use.\n\nVoltage measurement has the opposite problem. A typical meter input is about 10 MΩ. Measure the middle of two 1 MΩ resistors across 10 V (true 5.00 V):\n\n- 1 MΩ ‖ 10 MΩ = 0.909 MΩ\n- Reading = 10 V × 0.909 / (1 + 0.909) = 4.76 V, which is 4.8% low\n\nFix: use a higher current range for lower burden, measure the drop across a known resistor instead, or keep voltage measurements to nodes with less than about 100 kΩ of source resistance."},{"t":"Scope probes: the 10× design, compensation and bandwidth","body":"A 10× probe puts a 9 MΩ resistor in series with the scope's 1 MΩ input. That divides the signal by 10 and presents 10 MΩ to the circuit. But the scope input also has capacitance C₂ (the input plus the cable, say 100 pF), and a divider with capacitance on one leg is frequency dependent.\n\nA small capacitor C₁ across the 9 MΩ fixes it. The divider is flat at all frequencies when both legs have the same time constant:\n\n= R₁ · C₁ = R₂ · C₂\n= C₁ = C₂ · R₂ / R₁ = 100 pF / 9 = 11.1 pF\n\nThe trimmer in the probe sets this. To adjust it, probe the scope's 1 kHz calibration square wave and turn the trimmer until the top is flat. Rounded corners mean undercompensated, an overshooting spike means overcompensated.\n\nWhat the circuit sees at high frequency is C₁ in series with C₂, which is C₂ / 10 = 10 pF, against 110 pF for a bare 1× probe and its cable. Driven from a 10 kΩ source:\n\n- 1× probe: f = 1 / (2π × 10 kΩ × 110 pF) = 145 kHz\n- 10× probe: f = 1 / (2π × 10 kΩ × 10 pF) = 1.59 MHz\n\nThe scope itself limits speed too. Its rise time is about 0.35 / bandwidth, so a 100 MHz scope rises in 3.5 ns. To read amplitude within 3%, keep the signal's frequency below about a quarter of the scope's bandwidth. The cost of 10×: the signal is 10× smaller, so noise matters more on small signals."},{"t":"Ground-loop hazards with a mains-earthed scope","body":"Most benchtop scopes connect the probe ground clip, and the BNC shells, to mains earth through the power cord. The clip is not a floating reference. It is a wire to the building's earth.\n\nThe consequences:\n\n- Clipping the ground to any point that is not at earth potential shorts that point to earth through the scope. On a mains-connected circuit that can cause a destructive fault.\n- A bench supply or computer also tied to earth makes a second path. The loop between the two earth connections picks up hum from mains wiring and magnetic fields, and shows up as a 50 or 60 Hz ripple on your trace that the circuit does not have.\n- Two probes on one scope share the same ground, so you cannot put each ground clip at a different voltage.\n\nWhat to do:\n\n- Connect each ground clip only to the circuit's own 0 V, and keep the ground lead short.\n- Never remove the earth pin from a scope plug. That makes the scope's case live if it fails.\n- Do not probe mains-connected circuits with an ordinary scope at all. Use an isolation transformer with proper training, a differential probe, or a battery-powered scope, and leave the work to someone qualified.\n- Hum on a trace that the circuit itself does not produce points to a loop. Reduce the loop area, or power the circuit from an isolated supply such as a battery."}]},
{"n":4,"title":"Real-World Components","short":"Parts","blurb":"Resistors, capacitors, batteries, switches, relays, fuses and wire, as you buy them.","big":"Schematics draw perfect parts. Real ones come in packages with ratings, tolerances and markings you need to read. This module is about choosing and identifying the parts on your bench: what the color bands and number codes mean, which capacitor type to grab, and what the ratings printed on a switch or relay are telling you.","ideas":["Every part has ratings besides its main value. Resistors have power ratings, capacitors have voltage ratings, wires and switches have current ratings.","Resistor values come in standard series. E12 has 12 values per decade: 10, 12, 15, 18, 22, 27, 33, 39, 47, 56, 68, 82.","The 4-band color code is two digits, a multiplier and a tolerance band.","Ceramic capacitors suit small values and decoupling. Electrolytic capacitors hold a lot of charge but are polarized. Film capacitors are stable and precise.","Ceramic capacitors are often marked with a 3-digit code: two digits followed by the number of zeros, in picofarads.","Battery capacity is given in milliamp-hours (mAh). The battery's internal resistance limits how much current it can deliver at once.","Switches are described by poles and throws: SPST, SPDT, DPDT. Mechanical contacts bounce for a few milliseconds when they close.","A relay is a switch flipped by an electromagnet. Driving its coil from a transistor needs a diode across the coil.","A fuse is the cheapest protection there is. Choose one rated somewhat above the normal operating current."],"formulas":[["value = (d₁d₂) × 10ᵐ","4-band resistor code"],["104 → 10 × 10⁴ pF = 100 nF","Capacitor code"],["t ≈ capacity (mAh) / I (mA)","Rough battery runtime in hours"]],"tip":"An electrolytic capacitor fitted backwards, or run above its voltage rating, can bulge or burst. The stripe on the can marks the negative lead.","labs":["color","capcode"],"quiz":[{"q":"A resistor's bands read brown, black, orange, gold. Its value is:","o":["1 kΩ ±5%","10 kΩ ±5%","100 kΩ ±10%","103 Ω ±5%"],"a":1,"w":"Brown = 1, black = 0, orange = × 1000, gold = ±5%. So it is 10 kΩ."},{"q":"A ceramic capacitor marked 104 is:","o":["104 pF","10 nF","100 nF","1 µF"],"a":2,"w":"10 followed by four zeros pF = 100,000 pF = 100 nF."},{"q":"Why does a relay coil driven by a transistor need a diode across it?","o":["To make it switch faster","To absorb the voltage spike when the coil turns off","To lower its resistance","To let it run on AC"],"a":1,"w":"A coil resists sudden changes in current by producing a large reverse voltage. The diode gives that current a safe path."},{"q":"A DPDT switch has:","o":["One pole and two throws","Two poles, each with two throws","Two poles, each with one throw","Four poles"],"a":1,"w":"Double pole, double throw: two separate switches that move together, each with two positions."},{"q":"A 2000 mAh battery powers a gadget drawing 100 mA. Roughly how long will it last?","o":["2 hours","20 hours","200 hours","0.05 hours"],"a":1,"w":"2000 / 100 = 20 hours. Real life is usually a bit less because capacity falls at higher currents."},{"q":"Which capacitor type is polarized and must go in the right way round?","o":["Ceramic","Film","Aluminum electrolytic","Mica"],"a":2,"w":"Aluminum electrolytics, and tantalums too, are polarized. Ceramic, film and mica capacitors are not."}],"reading":"pp. 253–400","part":"A","ex":[{"q":"A resistor reads orange, orange, red, gold. You measure 3.52 kΩ across it. Is the part within tolerance?","steps":["Digits: orange = 3, orange = 3, giving 33. Multiplier: red = × 100. Value = 33 × 100 Ω = 3.3 kΩ.","Gold means ±5%, and 5% of 3.3 kΩ = 165 Ω.","Allowed range: 3.3 kΩ − 165 Ω = 3.135 kΩ up to 3.3 kΩ + 165 Ω = 3.465 kΩ.","3.52 kΩ is above 3.465 kΩ, so the part is out of tolerance. Parallel paths in a circuit can only lower a reading, never raise it, so a high reading points to the part itself (or a poor probe contact). Lift one leg and measure again to be sure."],"ans":"3.3 kΩ ±5%, range 3.135 to 3.465 kΩ. 3.52 kΩ is out of tolerance."},{"q":"A sensor runs from a 2000 mAh cell. It draws 80 mA for 1 s awake, then 0.1 mA for 9 s asleep, repeating. How long does the cell last if only 80% of its rated capacity is usable?","steps":["Average current over a 10 s cycle: (80 mA × 1 s + 0.1 mA × 9 s) / 10 s = 80.9 mA·s / 10 s = 8.09 mA.","Usable capacity: 0.80 × 2000 mAh = 1600 mAh.","Runtime: t = 1600 mAh / 8.09 mA = 198 h, which is 8.2 days.","Check the idea: the 1 s awake burst is 10% of the time but 99% of the charge (80 mA·s of 80.9 mA·s). Shortening the awake time is what extends the life."],"ans":"Average 8.09 mA, so about 198 hours (8.2 days)."}],"deep":[{"t":"E-series and tolerance stacking","body":"Each E-series divides a decade into equal ratio steps. E12 has 12 steps, so the ratio between neighbours is 10^(1/12) = 1.21. With ±10% tolerance, a 10 and a 12 part overlap (10 can reach 11, 12 can reach 10.8), so every value in the decade is covered by some part. E24 uses steps of 1.10 for ±5%, and E96 uses steps of 1.024 for ±1%.\n\nA tolerance only bounds one part. When parts work together, the errors add in the worst case.\n\nExample: a divider of two 10 kΩ ±1% resistors from 5 V. The ideal output is 2.5 V.\n\n- Worst high: R₂ = 10.1 kΩ, R₁ = 9.9 kΩ gives 5 V × 10.1 / 20.0 = 2.525 V\n- Worst low: R₂ = 9.9 kΩ, R₁ = 10.1 kΩ gives 5 V × 9.9 / 20.0 = 2.475 V\n\nThe output is within ±1% (±25 mV) of ideal, as good as one resistor.  Series strings behave the same way: the worst-case sum of ±5% parts is ±5% of the total, not worse. Absolute quantities (a timing RC, where both R and C directly set the answer) are different: ±1% on R and ±10% on C stack to ±11%.\n\nWhere the model breaks: tolerance is quoted at 25 °C and at delivery. Temperature coefficient, ageing and soldering add to it, so design with some margin."},{"t":"Ceramic capacitors lose capacitance under DC bias","body":"Ceramic capacitors fall into two families. C0G (also NP0) uses a stable dielectric: ±30 ppm/°C, no change with voltage, no ageing. Its limit is size: above about 100 nF the parts become large and costly. X7R, X5R and the like pack far more capacitance into a small case, but their dielectric is ferroelectric, and that costs you in three ways.\n\n- Temperature code: X7R means −55 °C to +125 °C with at most ±15% change over that range\n- DC bias: the applied voltage saturates the dielectric and the effective capacitance falls, often by half or more at a part's rated voltage\n- Ageing: a few percent per decade of hours, reset by heating above 125 °C\n\nThe bias loss is worst in small cases at high ratings and is quoted nowhere on the label. It is in the manufacturer's DC-bias curve. As an illustration, if a 10 µF 6.3 V X7R in a small case keeps 40% of its value at 5 V, the circuit sees 4 µF. To get 10 µF effective you would need 25 µF nominal, or a larger case, or a part with a higher voltage rating run at a smaller fraction of it.\n\nWhere it matters: regulator output capacitors (stability and load-step response), ADC reference pins and timing. Use C0G for filters and timing, and X7R for decoupling where a 2× error in capacitance is harmless."},{"t":"Resistor noise and voltage rating","body":"Every resistor generates thermal noise whose voltage depends on resistance, temperature and bandwidth:\n\n= vₙ = √(4 · k · T · R · B)\n\nwith k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J/K. At 300 K the factor 4kT is 1.66 × 10⁻²⁰.\n\n- 10 kΩ over a 20 kHz audio bandwidth: √(1.66 × 10⁻²⁰ × 10⁴ × 2 × 10⁴) = 1.8 µV rms\n- 1 MΩ over the same bandwidth: 18 µV rms, ten times more because noise scales with √R\n\nThat is why input stages of microphone and sensor amplifiers use low-value resistors. Film resistors add a little excess noise on top, and carbon composition adds more.\n\nResistors also have a maximum working voltage that is separate from the power rating. The usable voltage is the lower of the two limits:\n\n= V_max = min(V_rated, √(P · R))\n\nTake a ¼ W resistor rated 250 V. At 10 kΩ the power limit allows √(0.25 W × 10,000 Ω) = 50 V. At 1 MΩ the power limit would allow 500 V, but the part flashes over at 250 V, so the voltage rating wins. The crossover is R = V² / P = 250 kΩ. This matters in high-value dividers: for high voltages, split the resistance over several resistors in series so each carries only a part of the voltage."}]},
{"n":5,"title":"Capacitors, Inductors and Time","short":"RC · Time","blurb":"How capacitors and inductors store energy, and how RC circuits set timing.","big":"Resistors respond instantly. Capacitors and inductors don't, because they store energy. That delay is what makes timing, smoothing and filtering possible. The single most useful idea here is the RC time constant, τ = R × C, which tells you how fast a capacitor charges through a resistor.","ideas":["A capacitor stores charge on two plates separated by an insulator. It resists sudden changes in voltage.","Charged and left in a DC circuit, a capacitor behaves like an open circuit.","An inductor stores energy in a magnetic field. It resists sudden changes in current.","Through a resistor, a capacitor charges to 63% of the supply in one time constant τ = R × C. It is about 99% charged after 5τ.","Discharging follows the same curve in reverse. After one τ, 37% of the voltage is left.","Capacitors in parallel add. Capacitors in series combine like resistors in parallel.","Placing a 100 nF capacitor right next to each chip's power pins (decoupling) prevents a surprising number of mystery bugs."],"formulas":[["τ = R · C","RC time constant"],["V(t) = V · (1 − e^(−t/τ))","Charging capacitor"],["V(t) = V · e^(−t/τ)","Discharging capacitor"],["Q = C · V","Charge stored"],["E = ½ · C · V²","Energy stored in a capacitor"],["τ = L / R","RL time constant"]],"tip":"Need a delay of about one second? 100 kΩ and 10 µF gives τ = 1 s. That's a handy pair to remember.","labs":["rc"],"quiz":[{"q":"A capacitor in a DC circuit, once it has fully charged, behaves like:","o":["A short circuit","An open circuit","A resistor","An inductor"],"a":1,"w":"After charging, no more current flows into it, so it looks like a break in the circuit."},{"q":"R = 10 kΩ and C = 100 µF. Roughly how long until the capacitor is fully charged?","o":["1 ms","0.1 s","1 s","5 s"],"a":3,"w":"τ = 10,000 × 0.0001 = 1 s. Full charge takes about 5τ, so about 5 s."},{"q":"After one time constant, a charging capacitor has reached about:","o":["37%","50%","63%","99%"],"a":2,"w":"1 − e⁻¹ ≈ 0.63."},{"q":"Two 10 µF capacitors in parallel give:","o":["5 µF","10 µF","20 µF","100 µF"],"a":2,"w":"Capacitors in parallel add."},{"q":"An inductor mainly resists sudden changes in:","o":["Voltage","Current","Resistance","Frequency"],"a":1,"w":"Its magnetic field opposes any change in the current flowing through it."}],"reading":"pp. 94–155, 223–235","part":"A","ex":[{"q":"An enable pin switches on when its input passes 70% of a 3.3 V supply. It is fed from a 100 kΩ and 1 µF RC network, charging from 0 V. How long is the delay, and which R gives about 500 ms?","steps":["Time constant: τ = R · C = 100 kΩ × 1 µF = 100 ms.","Charging: V/Vs = 1 − e^(−t/τ) = 0.70, so e^(−t/τ) = 0.30. Then t = −τ · ln(0.30) = 100 ms × 1.204 = 120 ms. The pin trips at 0.70 × 3.3 V = 2.31 V.","For 500 ms with C = 1 µF: τ = 500 ms / 1.204 = 415 ms, so R = 415 ms / 1 µF = 415 kΩ.","Standard values: 390 kΩ gives 390 ms × 1.204 = 470 ms, and 430 kΩ gives 430 ms × 1.204 = 518 ms. Pick 430 kΩ if the delay must not be shorter than 500 ms. Capacitor tolerance will move it by ±10% or more."],"ans":"120 ms with 100 kΩ. For about 500 ms use 430 kΩ (518 ms) or 390 kΩ (470 ms)."},{"q":"A 1000 µF capacitor is charged to 12 V and then discharged through a 100 Ω load. Find the charge and energy stored, the starting current, and the time for the voltage to fall to 6 V.","steps":["Charge: Q = C · V = 1000 µF × 12 V = 12 mC.","Energy: E = ½ · C · V² = ½ × 1000 µF × (12 V)² = 72 mJ.","Starting current: I = V / R = 12 V / 100 Ω = 120 mA. Time constant: τ = R · C = 100 Ω × 1000 µF = 100 ms.","Discharging: V(t) = 12 V × e^(−t/τ) = 6 V when e^(−t/τ) = ½, so t = τ · ln 2 = 100 ms × 0.693 = 69.3 ms.","Check: at 6 V the capacitor holds ½ × 1000 µF × (6 V)² = 18 mJ, a quarter of the original, because energy goes as V²."],"ans":"12 mC and 72 mJ stored, 120 mA at the start, 69.3 ms to fall to 6 V."}],"deep":[{"t":"Where the exponential charging curve comes from","body":"A capacitor charged through a resistor from a supply V obeys Kirchhoff's voltage law. The current through R is the same as the current into C:\n\n= (V − v) / R = C · dv/dt\n\nThis says the charging speed is proportional to the voltage still missing, V − v. Separate the variables and integrate from v = 0 at t = 0:\n\n= dv / (V − v) = dt / (R · C)\n= −ln(V − v) + ln V = t / (R · C)\n= v(t) = V · (1 − e^(−t/τ)), with τ = R · C\n\nThe fractions reached are worth remembering:\n\n- 1τ: 63.2%\n- 2τ: 86.5%\n- 3τ: 95.0%\n- 5τ: 99.3%\n\nSolving for time gives t = −τ · ln(1 − fraction). The 10% to 90% rise time is τ · ln 9 = 2.2 τ. Since a low-pass filter with this RC has a cutoff f = 1 / (2πτ), the rise time and bandwidth are linked: t_rise = 2.2 / (2π · f) ≈ 0.35 / f.\n\nIf the initial current V / R stayed constant, the capacitor would reach V in exactly one τ. It doesn't, because the current decays as the capacitor fills."},{"t":"Inductor energy and the switch-off spike","body":"An inductor stores energy in its magnetic field, and the voltage across it follows how fast the current changes:\n\n= E = ½ · L · I²\n= v = L · di/dt\n\nExample: a 12 V relay coil of 400 Ω carries 12 V / 400 Ω = 30 mA. Say its inductance is 0.5 H.\n\n- Stored energy: ½ × 0.5 H × (30 mA)² = 0.225 mJ\n- If a switch cut the current to zero in 1 µs, the coil would try to produce v = 0.5 H × 30 mA / 1 µs = 15 kV\n\nIn practice the voltage rises until something breaks down: an arc across the switch contacts, or the junction of the transistor driving it. A diode across the coil (a flyback diode, cathode to the supply side) gives the current a path instead. Then the current decays through the coil's own resistance with τ = L / R = 0.5 H / 400 Ω = 1.25 ms, and the transistor sees only the supply voltage plus the diode drop.\n\nThe trade-off: the diode makes the relay release more slowly. Adding a Zener in series with the diode lets the voltage rise a little and shortens the decay.\n\nThe same law works in your favour in a boost converter, which switches an inductor on purpose to make an output higher than its input."},{"t":"ESR and ESL: real capacitors have a series resistor and inductor","body":"A real capacitor behaves like an ideal C in series with a small resistance (ESR) and a small inductance (ESL, from the leads and the internal structure).\n\nESL sets a self-resonant frequency, above which the part acts as an inductor:\n\n= f₀ = 1 / (2π · √(ESL · C))\n\nFor a 100 nF ceramic with 1 nH of ESL: f₀ = 1 / (2π × √(10⁻⁹ H × 10⁻⁷ F)) = 1 / (2π × 10⁻⁸ s) = 15.9 MHz. Above that, adding a bigger capacitor does not help; a smaller one with lower ESL, placed close to the pins, does. That is why decoupling networks often mix a 100 nF with a 10 µF.\n\nESR matters mostly for electrolytics. Take a 1000 µF capacitor with ESR = 0.1 Ω:\n\n- A sudden 1 A load step makes the voltage jump by 1 A × 0.1 Ω = 0.1 V at once. The ideal-capacitor droop at 1 A is I / C = 1 V/ms, so the ESR step equals 100 µs of discharge\n- 1 A rms of ripple current dissipates I² · R = 0.1 W inside the can, which heats it and shortens its life\n\nIn power supplies, the ESR term often matters more than I / (2fC) for ripple. Low-ESR and polymer types exist for that reason."}]},
{"n":6,"title":"Diodes","short":"Diodes","blurb":"One-way valves for current: rectifiers, Schottky diodes, Zeners and protection.","big":"A diode lets current flow one way and blocks it the other way. That one property gives you rectifiers that turn AC into DC, reverse-polarity protection, voltage clamps, and simple regulators. Each diode type trades off forward drop, speed and voltage rating, and the load-line method shows how a diode and a resistor settle on the current that actually flows.","ideas":["Current flows from anode to cathode. The stripe on the body marks the cathode.","A silicon diode drops about 0.6–0.7 V when it conducts. Schottky diodes drop about 0.2–0.4 V and switch faster.","In reverse, a diode blocks until it reaches its breakdown voltage.","A Zener diode is designed to break down at a precise voltage, so it can hold a voltage steady.","A diode in series with a power input protects a circuit from a battery put in backwards, at the cost of its forward drop.","The diode's I–V curve and the resistor's load line cross at the operating point. That point is the voltage and current you'd measure.","TVS diodes absorb fast voltage spikes to protect inputs."],"formulas":[["P = Vf · I","Diode power"],["I = (Vs − Vf) / R","Current through a diode and resistor"],["R = (Vs − Vz) / I","Zener series resistor"]],"tip":"Check a diode with a multimeter on its diode setting. One direction reads about 0.5–0.7 V. The other reads OL (open).","labs":["diode"],"quiz":[{"q":"A forward-biased silicon diode that is conducting drops about:","o":["0 V","0.2 V","0.7 V","5 V"],"a":2,"w":"Silicon PN junctions drop roughly 0.6–0.7 V when conducting."},{"q":"A Zener diode is normally used:","o":["Forward-biased, as a rectifier","Reverse-biased, to hold a fixed voltage","As an amplifier","To store charge"],"a":1,"w":"Zeners are built to break down at a precise reverse voltage and hold it there."},{"q":"Which diode has the lowest forward voltage drop?","o":["Silicon rectifier","Schottky","Red LED","Zener in reverse"],"a":1,"w":"Schottky diodes typically drop 0.2–0.4 V."},{"q":"The stripe on a diode's body marks:","o":["The anode","The cathode","Its voltage rating","Nothing in particular"],"a":1,"w":"The stripe marks the cathode. Conventional current flows toward it."},{"q":"A 5.1 V Zener runs from 12 V with 10 mA through it. The series resistor should be about:","o":["510 Ω","690 Ω","1.2 kΩ","69 Ω"],"a":1,"w":"R = (12 − 5.1) / 0.01 = 690 Ω. The nearest standard value is 680 Ω."}],"reading":"pp. 407–429, 481–491","part":"A","ex":[{"q":"A 5.1 V Zener regulates a rail from 12 V. The load draws up to 15 mA, and the Zener needs at least 5 mA to stay in regulation. Choose the resistor and check the ratings.","steps":["At full load the resistor must carry 15 mA + 5 mA = 20 mA. It drops 12 V − 5.1 V = 6.9 V.","R = 6.9 V / 20 mA = 345 Ω at most. A larger R would leave less than 5 mA for the Zener. Round down to a standard 330 Ω.","Total current: I = 6.9 V / 330 Ω = 20.9 mA. At full load the Zener carries 20.9 mA − 15 mA = 5.9 mA, which is above 5 mA.","With the load removed, the Zener takes all 20.9 mA: P = 5.1 V × 20.9 mA = 107 mW. A 500 mW part has plenty of margin.","Resistor power: 6.9 V × 20.9 mA = 144 mW. A ¼ W resistor is at 58% of its rating, so ½ W would run cooler."],"ans":"330 Ω. Zener current 5.9 mA at full load, 20.9 mA unloaded (107 mW). Resistor 144 mW."},{"q":"A circuit draws 300 mA from a 9 V battery through a series diode for reverse-polarity protection. Compare a silicon rectifier diode that drops 0.8 V at that current with a Schottky diode that drops 0.35 V.","steps":["Silicon: the circuit gets 9 V − 0.8 V = 8.2 V. Diode power: 0.8 V × 300 mA = 240 mW.","Schottky: the circuit gets 9 V − 0.35 V = 8.65 V. Diode power: 0.35 V × 300 mA = 105 mW.","Fraction of battery power lost: silicon 240 mW / (9 V × 300 mA = 2.7 W) = 8.9%, Schottky 105 mW / 2.7 W = 3.9%.","Both block a battery put in backwards, since the diode is reverse-biased. The Schottky gives 0.45 V more to the circuit, which matters as the battery runs down. Check the datasheet: Schottky reverse leakage is higher and grows with temperature, and the reverse voltage rating must exceed the battery voltage."],"ans":"Silicon: 8.2 V to the circuit, 240 mW lost. Schottky: 8.65 V, 105 mW lost."}],"deep":[{"t":"The Shockley equation and the −2 mV/°C tempco","body":"The current through an ideal junction diode follows:\n\n= I = Is · (e^(V / (n · V_T)) − 1)\n\nIs is the saturation current (tiny, about 10⁻¹⁵ to 10⁻⁹ A, and strongly temperature dependent), n is an ideality factor between 1 and 2, and V_T = kT/q is the thermal voltage, 25.9 mV at 300 K.\n\nBecause of the exponential, each tenfold increase in current needs the same added voltage:\n\n= ΔV per decade = n · V_T · ln 10 = 59.5 mV × n at 300 K\n\nIf a diode shows 0.60 V at 1 mA, it shows roughly 0.66 V at 10 mA and 0.72 V at 100 mA (taking n = 1). That is why \"0.7 V\" is a good guess over a huge range of currents. The slope also gives the small-signal resistance:\n\n= r_d = n · V_T / I\n\nThat is 25.9 Ω at 1 mA and 2.6 Ω at 10 mA (n = 1).\n\nTemperature raises Is much faster than it raises V_T, so at constant current the forward voltage falls by about 2 mV per °C. Over 50 °C, that is 0.60 V − 0.10 V = 0.50 V. Two consequences:\n\n- A diode makes a cheap temperature sensor if you hold the current fixed\n- Paralleled diodes or transistors can run away: the hotter one takes more current, which heats it further. Add a small series resistor to each."},{"t":"Reverse recovery: why Schottky diodes switch faster","body":"While a PN diode conducts, minority carriers pile up on both sides of the junction. When the voltage reverses, that stored charge must be swept out before the diode blocks. For a short time the diode conducts backwards, a reverse current that peaks and then decays over the reverse recovery time t_rr.\n\n- A small-signal diode such as the 1N4148 has t_rr of a few nanoseconds\n- General-purpose rectifier diodes such as the 1N4001 to 1N4007 are far slower, in the microseconds (check the datasheet)\n- Fast-recovery and ultrafast diodes sit in between, for switching supplies\n\nExample: in a buck converter running at 100 kHz, the period is 10 µs. A diode with t_rr = 2 µs conducts backwards for 20% of every cycle, wasting energy and making noise. A mains-frequency rectifier at 50 Hz has a 20 ms period and does not care.\n\nA Schottky diode (metal-semiconductor junction) conducts with majority carriers only, so there is almost no stored charge. It switches in a few nanoseconds and drops 0.2 to 0.4 V.\n\nThe costs: higher reverse leakage that rises steeply with temperature, and lower reverse voltage ratings (silicon types mostly top out around 100 to 200 V; silicon carbide parts go much higher). Pick Schottky for low-voltage switching supplies and reverse-polarity protection, and PN or fast-recovery types for high-voltage rectification."},{"t":"Zener versus TVS","body":"Both are diodes operated in reverse breakdown, but they are built for different jobs.\n\nA Zener is a steady-state voltage reference. Its voltage is specified at a small test current (often 5 mA), with a tolerance of ±5% or so and a dynamic impedance Zz that tells you how much the voltage moves with current. Below roughly 5 V the breakdown is dominated by tunnelling and the voltage falls slightly with temperature. Above roughly 6 V, avalanche dominates and it rises with temperature. Near 5 to 6 V the effects cancel, which is why 5.1 V Zeners are popular as references.\n\nA TVS (transient voltage suppressor) is built to survive huge short pulses. It is rated by peak pulse power, for example 600 W for a 10/1000 µs pulse, not by steady power. Its specifications:\n\n- Stand-off voltage V_RWM: the highest normal working voltage, with leakage still low\n- Breakdown voltage: where it starts to conduct, a little above V_RWM\n- Clamping voltage V_C at the peak pulse current I_PP: the voltage the circuit actually sees during the surge\n\nA typical 600 W part with 12 V stand-off clamps at about 19.9 V at 30 A (19.9 V × 30 A ≈ 600 W). Choose V_RWM at or above the highest voltage the rail normally reaches, and make sure everything downstream survives V_C.\n\nA Zener cannot do this job: its junction is too small, and a surge much above its power rating destroys it. Use a Zener to set a voltage, and a TVS to stop a spike."}]},
{"n":7,"title":"Transistors","short":"Transistors","blurb":"BJTs and MOSFETs as switches, and a first look at amplifiers.","big":"A transistor lets a small signal control a much larger current. For most builders the key skill is using one as a switch, which is how a microcontroller pin that can supply only a few milliamps drives a motor, a relay or an LED strip. Learn how to saturate a BJT, when to use a logic-level MOSFET instead, and why low-side switching is the easy default.","ideas":["A bipolar transistor (BJT) is current-controlled. A small base current allows a collector current about hFE times larger.","To use a BJT as a switch, push enough base current to saturate it. Vce then drops to about 0.2 V and it wastes little power.","Design the base current for a gain of about 10, even if the datasheet says hFE is 100. That covers variation between parts and temperature.","A MOSFET is voltage-controlled. Its gate draws almost no steady current.","Logic-level MOSFETs turn fully on with only 3.3–5 V on the gate, and they're the default choice for switching big loads.","NPN and N-channel parts usually go on the low side, between the load and ground. PNP and P-channel parts go on the high side.","Add a pull-down resistor (around 100 kΩ) from a MOSFET's gate to ground so it stays off while the microcontroller boots.","Thyristors (SCRs and TRIACs) latch on once triggered. They are used to switch AC loads."],"formulas":[["Ic ≈ hFE · Ib","BJT current gain"],["Rb = (Vin − 0.7) / Ib","Base resistor for a switch"],["P = Vce(sat) · Ic","BJT switch dissipation"],["P = I² · Rds(on)","MOSFET switch dissipation"]],"labs":["bjt"],"quiz":[{"q":"What's the best default part for letting a 3.3 V microcontroller switch a 2 A, 12 V load?","o":["A small-signal NPN like the 2N3904","A logic-level N-channel MOSFET","A Zener diode","A TRIAC"],"a":1,"w":"A 2N3904 is rated for about 200 mA. A logic-level MOSFET turns fully on from 3.3 V and handles amps with a tiny voltage drop."},{"q":"A saturated BJT switch has a collector-emitter voltage of about:","o":["0.2 V","0.7 V","Half the supply","The full supply"],"a":0,"w":"Fully on, Vce(sat) is typically 0.1–0.3 V."},{"q":"A BJT switch must carry 100 mA. Designing for a forced gain of 10, the base current is:","o":["1 mA","10 mA","100 mA","0.1 mA"],"a":1,"w":"Ib = 100 mA / 10 = 10 mA."},{"q":"With a 5 V drive and 10 mA of base current, the base resistor is about:","o":["43 Ω","430 Ω","4.3 kΩ","500 Ω"],"a":1,"w":"(5 − 0.7) / 0.01 = 430 Ω. The nearest E12 value below is 390 Ω, which gives a little extra margin."},{"q":"A MOSFET with Rds(on) = 0.05 Ω carries 3 A. It dissipates:","o":["0.15 W","0.45 W","1.5 W","4.5 W"],"a":1,"w":"P = I² × R = 9 × 0.05 = 0.45 W."},{"q":"Why add a pull-down resistor on a MOSFET gate?","o":["To speed it up","To keep it off while the driving pin is floating","To raise Rds(on)","To protect against reverse voltage"],"a":1,"w":"A floating gate can drift on, randomly switching the load. The pull-down holds it at 0 V until the pin takes control."}],"reading":"pp. 429–481","part":"A","ex":[{"q":"A 5 V microcontroller pin switches an 80 mA, 12 V relay coil through an NPN transistor. Choose the base resistor with a forced gain of 10 and check the numbers.","steps":["Base current for forced gain 10: Ib = Ic / 10 = 80 mA / 10 = 8 mA.","Rb = (Vin − Vbe) / Ib = (5 V − 0.7 V) / 8 mA = 538 Ω.","Standard values: E12 has 470 Ω and 560 Ω. Take 470 Ω, the value below, so that the base current is a little higher than needed: Ib = 4.3 V / 470 Ω = 9.15 mA, a forced gain of 80 mA / 9.15 mA = 8.7.","The pin supplies 9.15 mA, which is within the typical rating of a microcontroller pin. Check your part's limit.","Transistor dissipation: P = Vce(sat) · Ic = 0.2 V × 80 mA = 16 mW. Put a diode across the coil, cathode to 12 V, to absorb the turn-off spike."],"ans":"Rb = 470 Ω. Ib = 9.15 mA, forced gain 8.7, transistor dissipation 16 mW."},{"q":"A 3.3 V microcontroller must switch a 12 V, 3 A load. Why not a BJT, and what does a logic-level MOSFET with Rds(on) = 50 mΩ cost in heat?","steps":["BJT with forced gain 10: Ib = 3 A / 10 = 300 mA. A 3.3 V pin cannot supply that, and a Darlington drops more than 1 V.","BJT loss would be Vce(sat) · Ic = 0.2 V × 3 A = 0.6 W, plus the base drive.","The MOSFET gate draws no steady current, so the pin drives it directly. Use a part whose Rds(on) is specified at Vgs = 3.3 V or lower, not just at 10 V.","MOSFET loss: P = I² · Rds(on) = (3 A)² × 50 mΩ = 9 A² × 50 mΩ = 0.45 W. The voltage across it is 3 A × 50 mΩ = 0.15 V.","In a package with θJA = 60 °C/W the temperature rise is 0.45 W × 60 °C/W = 27 °C. Add a 100 kΩ gate pull-down so the load stays off while the pin is floating."],"ans":"BJT needs 300 mA of base current. MOSFET: 0.45 W, a 27 °C rise at 60 °C/W."}],"deep":[{"t":"Gain spread: design for the minimum","body":"The datasheet gives hFE as a range, not a number. A 2N3904 at 10 mA and Vce = 1 V is specified from 100 to 300, a 3:1 spread, and the gain also changes with collector current and temperature. At 100 mA the minimum falls to about 30.\n\nIn a switch, design with the minimum gain and then divide it again for margin, which is what the forced gain of 10 does. With Ic = 100 mA and Ib = 10 mA, the transistor only needs β = 10 to carry the current. Its guaranteed minimum is about 30 at 100 mA, so even the weakest part has 3× to spare, and at 10 mA (minimum 100) it has 10×.\n\nIn a linear amplifier you cannot overdrive. Say the base is fed with a fixed 20 µA:\n\n- Minimum β = 100 gives Ic = 2 mA\n- Typical β = 200 gives Ic = 4 mA\n- Maximum β = 300 gives Ic = 6 mA\n\nThe collector current, and with it the output bias, varies by 3:1 between individual parts. This is why linear stages use emitter degeneration and bias networks that fix the collector current with resistors, so that β drops out:\n\n= Ic ≈ (V_B − 0.7 V) / R_E\n\nThe base voltage V_B is set by a stiff divider and R_E is the emitter resistor. The circuit then depends on two resistors, not on a parameter that varies 3:1."},{"t":"Saturation, and why we overdrive the base","body":"A transistor in the active region keeps Ic = β · Ib and a collector-emitter voltage set by the circuit. In saturation, the base is driven so hard that the collector-base junction also conducts, Vce falls to a few tenths of a volt and Ic is set by the load, not by β.\n\nDatasheets quote Vce(sat) at a stated forced gain, nearly always 10. For a 2N3904, 0.2 V maximum is guaranteed at Ic = 10 mA with Ib = 1 mA. Starve the base and Vce(sat) rises and the transistor leaves saturation.\n\nExample: a transistor carries 100 mA.\n\n- Well saturated, Vce = 0.2 V: power = 0.2 V × 100 mA = 20 mW\n- Starved so that Vce = 2 V: power = 2 V × 100 mA = 200 mW\n\nThe load voltage also falls, and the dissipation is ten times higher in a small package. A transistor in the active region is a variable resistor and wastes the supply as heat.\n\nThere is a trade-off. Heavy overdrive stores charge in the base, and the transistor then takes longer to turn off (storage time). In slow switching this is irrelevant. For fast logic and switchers it is why Schottky clamps and MOSFETs exist. Forced gain 10 is the compromise: deep enough to saturate, light enough to turn off quickly."},{"t":"BJT or MOSFET as a switch","body":"Compare the two when fully on.\n\n- BJT loss: P = Vce(sat) · Ic. Vce(sat) has a floor around 0.1 to 0.3 V no matter how large the part is, plus the base drive power\n- MOSFET loss: P = Ic² · Rds(on), which falls as the square of the current falls and can be milliohms in large parts, with no steady gate current\n\nThe MOSFET wins when Ic · Rds(on) < Vce(sat), that is when Rds(on) < 0.2 V / Ic. At 3 A that is 67 mΩ, so a 50 mΩ part beats a BJT. At 10 mA, 0.2 V / 10 mA = 20 Ω and any MOSFET wins on conduction loss, but a 2N3904 costs a few cents and needs no logic-level check, which is why BJTs survive in LED and relay drivers.\n\nThe gate is a capacitor, so it takes charge on every edge. For a gate charge Q_g = 10 nC:\n\n- At 100 kHz the average gate current is Q_g · f = 10 nC × 100 kHz = 1 mA\n- To switch in 50 ns the peak current is Q_g / t = 10 nC / 50 ns = 0.2 A\n\nA microcontroller pin cannot give 0.2 A, so fast power stages use a gate driver. For slow switching (relays, LEDs, motors under PWM at a few kHz) the pin is enough.\n\nWhere the simple model breaks: Rds(on) rises with temperature, roughly doubling from 25 °C to 125 °C in many parts, so size the heat for the hot value. And Rds(on) is quoted at a given Vgs. A 'logic-level' part is the one that specifies it at 4.5 V or lower."}]},
{"n":8,"title":"LEDs and Light","short":"LEDs","blurb":"Driving LEDs safely, light sensors, and optoisolators.","big":"LEDs are the first thing most people light up, and the first thing most people burn out. An LED must have its current limited, usually by a series resistor chosen from the supply voltage, the LED's forward voltage and the current you want. This module also covers sensing light, with photoresistors, photodiodes and phototransistors, and sending signals across an isolation gap with an optoisolator.","ideas":["An LED needs current limiting. Without it, the current climbs until the LED fails.","Forward voltage depends on color. Red is about 1.8–2.2 V. Green, blue and white are about 2.8–3.4 V.","Small indicator LEDs are usually rated for 20–30 mA maximum, but most look bright at 2–10 mA.","Give every LED in parallel its own resistor. Slight differences in forward voltage make one LED take most of the current.","You can put LEDs in series if the supply is higher than their combined forward voltage plus some headroom for the resistor.","A photoresistor's resistance drops in light. Put it in a voltage divider to turn light level into a voltage.","Photodiodes and phototransistors respond much faster than photoresistors.","An optoisolator sends a signal across a gap with light, so the two circuits share no electrical connection."],"formulas":[["R = (Vs − Vf) / I","LED series resistor"],["P = (Vs − Vf) · I","Resistor power"],["R = (Vs − n·Vf) / I","n LEDs in series"]],"labs":["led"],"quiz":[{"q":"A red LED (Vf = 2 V) runs from 5 V at 15 mA. The series resistor should be:","o":["100 Ω","200 Ω","330 Ω","1 kΩ"],"a":1,"w":"R = (5 − 2) / 0.015 = 200 Ω. In practice you'd pick the next standard value up, 220 Ω."},{"q":"Why shouldn't two LEDs in parallel share one resistor?","o":["They would be dimmer","Small differences in Vf make one LED take most of the current","LEDs only work in series","It doubles the voltage"],"a":1,"w":"The LED with the lower forward voltage takes most of the current and can burn out."},{"q":"How many white LEDs (Vf ≈ 3.2 V each) can you run in series from 12 V with a resistor?","o":["1","3","4","6"],"a":1,"w":"Three need 9.6 V, which leaves 2.4 V for the resistor. Four would need 12.8 V, more than the supply."},{"q":"An optoisolator is mainly used for:","o":["Making LEDs brighter","Electrical isolation between two circuits","Measuring light level","Storing data"],"a":1,"w":"It carries a signal across a gap with light, so the two sides have no electrical connection."},{"q":"A photoresistor in bright light has:","o":["High resistance","Low resistance","Infinite resistance","Negative resistance"],"a":1,"w":"More light frees more charge carriers, so its resistance falls."}],"reading":"pp. 495–524","part":"B","ex":[{"q":"Three green LEDs (Vf = 2.1 V each) are wired in series from 12 V at about 20 mA. Choose the resistor, then compare the efficiency with running the same three LEDs from 12 V on separate branches.","steps":["Combined forward voltage: 3 × 2.1 V = 6.3 V. The resistor drops 12 V − 6.3 V = 5.7 V.","R = 5.7 V / 20 mA = 285 Ω. Standard values: E24 has 300 Ω (19.0 mA), E12 has 270 Ω (21.1 mA, slightly over) and 330 Ω. Take 330 Ω to stay under 20 mA.","Current: I = 5.7 V / 330 Ω = 17.3 mA. Resistor power: I² · R = (17.3 mA)² × 330 Ω = 99 mW, fine for a ¼ W part.","Efficiency of the series string: LEDs use 6.3 V × 17.3 mA = 109 mW out of 12 V × 17.3 mA = 207 mW, so 53%.","Three separate branches, each LED with its own resistor, would draw three times the current from 12 V: 3 × 207 mW = 622 mW for the same light. The series string is about 3× more economical, as long as the supply has more voltage than the LEDs plus the resistor's drop."],"ans":"330 Ω, giving 17.3 mA. The string is 53% efficient, against about 18% for three separate branches."},{"q":"A photoresistor measures about 2 kΩ in bright light and 200 kΩ in the dark. Wired as a divider on 5 V, which fixed resistor gives the widest, most balanced swing, and what output voltages result?","steps":["Put the photoresistor on top and the fixed resistor to ground, so the output rises with light.","Choose the fixed resistor as the geometric mean of the two extremes: R = √(2 kΩ × 200 kΩ) = √(4 × 10⁸ Ω²) = 20 kΩ. This sits symmetrically between them on a ratio scale.","Bright: Vout = 5 V × 20 kΩ / (2 kΩ + 20 kΩ) = 5 V × 0.909 = 4.55 V.","Dark: Vout = 5 V × 20 kΩ / (200 kΩ + 20 kΩ) = 5 V × 0.0909 = 0.455 V.","A 4.1 V swing is more than most microcontroller pins need. Photoresistors are slow (tens of milliseconds) and differ a lot between parts, so use a threshold you can adjust or calibrate."],"ans":"20 kΩ, giving 4.55 V in bright light and 0.455 V in the dark."}],"deep":[{"t":"Why LEDs need current control, not voltage control","body":"An LED's current rises exponentially with voltage, like any diode: a change of about 0.1 V multiplies the current by e^(0.1 V / (n · V_T)). With n ≈ 2 and V_T = 25.9 mV, that is e^1.93 ≈ 7. Forward voltage also varies between parts of the same type, and it falls by roughly 2 mV per °C as the LED heats. A fixed voltage source therefore cannot set the current: one LED draws 5 mA and its neighbour 35 mA, and as they warm up the current climbs.\n\nA series resistor makes a stiff current source out of a voltage source. The current responds to a change in Vf by:\n\n= ΔI = ΔVf / R\n\nExample, red LED (Vf = 2.0 V) at 15 mA:\n\n- From 5 V, R = 200 Ω. If Vf drops by 0.1 V: ΔI = 0.1 V / 200 Ω = 0.5 mA, a 3% change\n- From 2.5 V, R = 33 Ω. The same 0.1 V change gives ΔI = 0.1 V / 33 Ω = 3 mA, a 20% change\n\nMore headroom gives better regulation but wastes more power, since the efficiency of the LED branch is about Vf / Vs (40% from 5 V, 80% from 2.5 V). When efficiency or brightness matching matters, such as in battery lamps or LED strips, use a constant-current driver instead of a resistor."},{"t":"Luminous efficacy, perceived brightness and binning","body":"Efficacy is the light output per watt of electrical input, in lumens per watt:\n\n= efficacy = luminous flux (lm) / electrical power (W)\n\nA lumen weights light by the eye's sensitivity, which peaks in green at 555 nm. The theoretical maximum is 683 lm/W for pure 555 nm light. White LEDs are well below that because blue light is converted to a broad spectrum and some energy always becomes heat. Modern white LEDs reach on the order of 100 to 200 lm/W; check the datasheet for your part.\n\nExample: a white LED at 350 mA and 3.0 V draws 1.05 W. At 120 lm/W it gives 126 lm, and the rest, about 0.9 W, heats the junction. Efficacy falls as junction temperature and current rise (droop), so a heatsink increases light as well as lifetime.\n\nEyes respond roughly like a power law, so perceived brightness grows more slowly than light output. Using an exponent of about 0.4: halving the current to half the light gives 0.5^0.4 = 76% of the perceived brightness. A tenth of the light looks like 0.1^0.4 = 40%. That is why a dimmer needs a wide range of current to look smooth.\n\nBinning: manufacturers sort LEDs after production by flux, forward voltage and colour. The same part number can differ by tens of percent in brightness. For matched appearance, order from one bin and run LEDs in series so that they carry exactly the same current."},{"t":"PWM dimming versus constant-current dimming","body":"Two ways to dim an LED:\n\n- Constant-current (analog) dimming lowers the current. It is simple and flicker-free, but the colour shifts slightly with current, and at low currents part-to-part variation is large\n- PWM dimming switches the LED between full current and zero. The eye averages the light, so the brightness follows the duty cycle D, and the colour stays correct because the LED always runs at its design current\n\nPWM sets the average current:\n\n= I_avg = D · I_peak\n\nExample: a 20 mA LED at D = 25% averages 5 mA. Using the 0.4 exponent for perception, that looks like 0.25^0.4 = 57% as bright, not 25%. For a perceptually linear dimmer the duty cycle must follow a curve, and low duty needs fine resolution.\n\nChoose the frequency with care. Above about 25 Hz a blinking LED looks steady to a still eye, but a moving eye or a camera sees banding at far higher rates. Use 1 kHz or more for lighting. Higher frequency also means more switching loss and EMI, so there is an upper limit.\n\nWhen PWM drives LEDs through a resistor, size the resistor for the peak current, since that is what flows during each pulse. Its average power falls with D: P_avg = D · I² · R."}]},
{"n":9,"title":"Power Supplies","short":"Power","blurb":"Rectifiers, smoothing, linear regulators and switching converters.","big":"Every project needs clean, stable power. The classic chain is transformer, then rectifier, then smoothing capacitor, then regulator. Linear regulators are simple and quiet but turn the excess voltage into heat. Switching converters are efficient but noisier. In practice you'll usually buy a USB adapter or a cheap buck module, but knowing how they work helps you choose and debug them.","ideas":["A bridge rectifier (four diodes) uses both halves of the AC wave. A single diode uses only one half.","A smoothing capacitor fills in the gaps between peaks. What's left is called ripple.","Full-wave ripple is about I / (2·f·C). A bigger capacitor or a lighter load means less ripple.","Linear regulator heat: P = (Vin − Vout) × I. Dropping 12 V to 5 V at 1 A wastes 7 W.","A 7805-style regulator needs about 2 V of headroom. Low-dropout (LDO) regulators need much less.","Put the capacitors the datasheet asks for on the regulator's input and output, or it may oscillate.","Buck converters step down at 85–95% efficiency. Boost converters step up."],"formulas":[["P = (Vin − Vout) · I","Linear regulator dissipation"],["η ≈ Vout / Vin","Linear regulator efficiency"],["Vripple ≈ I / (2·f·C)","Full-wave ripple"],["Vout = 1.25 · (1 + R2/R1)","LM317 adjustable output"],["ΔT = P · θJA","Temperature rise"]],"labs":["rectifier","reg"],"quiz":[{"q":"A 7805 regulates 9 V down to 5 V at 500 mA. It dissipates:","o":["0.5 W","2 W","2.5 W","4.5 W"],"a":1,"w":"(9 − 5) × 0.5 = 2 W, which needs a heatsink on a TO-220 package."},{"q":"Why choose a buck converter over a linear regulator?","o":["Lower noise","Much better efficiency when the voltage drop is large","Fewer parts","It works only at DC"],"a":1,"w":"A switching converter stays efficient whatever the drop. A linear regulator wastes the whole difference as heat."},{"q":"Doubling the smoothing capacitor in a rectified supply:","o":["Doubles the ripple","Halves the ripple","Has no effect","Doubles the output voltage"],"a":1,"w":"Ripple is roughly I / (2fC), so it falls in proportion to C."},{"q":"An LM317 with R1 = 240 Ω and R2 = 720 Ω outputs about:","o":["3.75 V","5 V","1.25 V","2.5 V"],"a":1,"w":"1.25 × (1 + 720/240) = 1.25 × 4 = 5 V."},{"q":"A full-wave rectifier on 60 Hz mains produces ripple at:","o":["30 Hz","60 Hz","120 Hz","240 Hz"],"a":2,"w":"Both halves of each cycle produce a peak, so the ripple is at twice the mains frequency."}],"reading":"pp. 699–716","part":"B","ex":[{"q":"A transformer secondary gives 9 V rms at 50 Hz and feeds a bridge rectifier, then a smoothing capacitor, for a 500 mA load. Find the peak DC, and choose a capacitor for about 1 V of ripple.","steps":["Peak of the secondary: 9 V × √2 = 12.73 V.","A bridge passes current through two diodes at a time: 12.73 V − 2 × 0.7 V = 11.33 V at the peak.","Ripple at 2 × 50 Hz = 100 Hz. For 1 V: C = I / (2·f·V) = 0.5 A / (100 Hz × 1 V) = 5000 µF. Use the standard 4700 µF.","With 4700 µF: ripple = 0.5 A / (100 Hz × 4700 µF) = 1.06 V. The output swings from 11.33 V down to 11.33 V − 1.06 V = 10.27 V.","Average ≈ 11.33 V − 0.53 V = 10.8 V. A 7805 would then burn (10.8 V − 5 V) × 0.5 A = 2.9 W, which is why a lower transformer voltage or a buck converter is attractive. The capacitor needs a voltage rating above 11.33 V plus mains variation, so use 25 V."],"ans":"Peak 11.33 V. Use 4700 µF for 1.06 V ripple (10.27 V minimum). Rating 25 V."},{"q":"A 7805-style regulator turns 12 V into 5 V at 400 mA, with θJA = 50 °C/W and no heatsink. Will it survive in a 25 °C room, and how does a 90% efficient buck converter compare?","steps":["Heat: P = (Vin − Vout) · I = (12 V − 5 V) × 0.4 A = 2.8 W. Efficiency ≈ Vout / Vin = 5 V / 12 V = 42%.","Temperature rise: ΔT = P · θJA = 2.8 W × 50 °C/W = 140 °C, so the junction reaches 25 °C + 140 °C = 165 °C. That is above the usual 125 to 150 °C limit, and the regulator will shut down or fail.","With a heatsink whose total thermal resistance (junction to case, pad, and sink to air) is 16 °C/W: ΔT = 2.8 W × 16 °C/W = 44.8 °C, so the junction sits at about 70 °C.","Buck converter at 90%: the input power is 5 V × 0.4 A / 0.9 = 2.22 W. The loss is 2.22 W − 2.0 W = 0.22 W, and the input current is 2.22 W / 12 V = 185 mA.","The linear regulator wastes 2.8 W to deliver 2.0 W. The buck wastes 0.22 W, about 13 times less."],"ans":"Linear: 2.8 W, 165 °C junction, fails unaided; about 70 °C with a 16 °C/W heatsink. Buck: 0.22 W lost."}],"deep":[{"t":"Deriving the ripple formula","body":"Between peaks, the diodes are off and the capacitor alone feeds the load. A load current I removes charge at a steady rate. Over a time Δt, the capacitor loses ΔQ = I · Δt, so the voltage falls by:\n\n= ΔV = I · Δt / C\n\nFor a full-wave rectifier the gap between peaks is half a mains cycle, Δt = 1 / (2f). So:\n\n= ΔV ≈ I / (2 · f · C)\n\nFor 50 Hz mains, the ripple is at 100 Hz and Δt = 10 ms. A 0.5 A load on 4700 µF droops 0.5 A × 10 ms / 4700 µF = 1.06 V. For half-wave rectification Δt is a full cycle, so you need twice the capacitance.\n\nThis is an upper bound, because the capacitor recharges for a short time at the top of each peak, so the discharge lasts slightly less than the full half-cycle. The bigger the capacitor, the shorter that conduction angle, and the closer the estimate gets.\n\nTwo more effects:\n\n- The diodes conduct only in short pulses to refill the capacitor, so their peak current is several times the load current. Rate the diodes and transformer for that, not for the average\n- ESR adds a step of I · ESR on top of the ripple, which can dominate for small, cheap capacitors"},{"t":"Dropout and the heat budget of a linear regulator","body":"A linear regulator needs an input some voltage above its output, the dropout voltage, to stay in regulation. A 7805 needs about 2 V of headroom, so the input must never fall below 7 V. A low-dropout (LDO) regulator needs 0.1 to 0.5 V, depending on the part and the load. When the input falls below Vout + Vdrop, the output follows it down, no longer regulated.\n\nThe regulator turns the difference into heat, with a small extra for its own supply current Iq:\n\n= P = (Vin − Vout) · I + Vin · Iq\n\nThe junction temperature must stay below its limit, so the heat budget gives the maximum current:\n\n= I_max = (Tj_max − T_ambient) / (θJA · (Vin − Vout))\n\nExample: 5 V to 3.3 V in a package with θJA = 60 °C/W, junction limit 125 °C, ambient 50 °C.\n\n- Allowed power: (125 °C − 50 °C) / 60 °C/W = 1.25 W\n- Drop: 5 V − 3.3 V = 1.7 V, so I_max = 1.25 W / 1.7 V = 735 mA\n- At 300 mA: P = 1.7 V × 0.3 A = 0.51 W and the rise is 0.51 W × 60 °C/W = 30.6 °C\n\nThe efficiency of a linear regulator is Vout / Vin, here 66%. In a battery device, an LDO whose input is only just above the output keeps that figure high and the quiescent current low."},{"t":"Where switching converters beat linear regulators","body":"A linear regulator passes all the load current and burns the voltage difference. A buck converter instead chops the input and filters it through an inductor, passing energy rather than burning it, so its loss depends on the converter, not on the voltage difference.\n\nExample: 12 V to 5 V at 1 A.\n\n- Linear: (12 V − 5 V) × 1 A = 7 W lost, an efficiency of 5 / 12 = 42%\n- Buck at 90%: input power 5 W / 0.9 = 5.56 W, so the loss is 0.56 W\n\nThe gap widens as the voltage ratio grows and the current rises. A switcher also offers things a linear regulator cannot: a boost converter makes a higher voltage than its input, and a buck-boost can go either way. That is what a battery that falls from 4.2 V to 3.0 V needs to feed a steady 3.3 V rail.\n\nLinear regulators still win in several places:\n\n- Small differences, where the efficiency is already high, such as 3.7 V to 3.3 V through an LDO (89%)\n- Tiny currents, where 12 V to 3.3 V at 50 mA wastes only 0.44 W and a converter's own quiescent current could cost more\n- Noise-sensitive circuits (audio, ADC references, RF), because a linear regulator adds no switching ripple at 100s of kHz and no radiated EMI\n- Cost and layout, since there is no inductor and no sensitive loop\n\nA common design puts a buck converter first to drop the voltage efficiently, then a low-dropout regulator to clean the output for the sensitive part."}]},
{"n":10,"title":"Oscillators and Timers","short":"Timers","blurb":"The 555 timer, RC oscillators and crystals.","big":"Oscillators make the repeating signals behind blinking LEDs, tones, clocks and PWM. The 555 timer is the classic. Wired as an astable, it free-runs at a frequency set by two resistors and a capacitor. Wired as a monostable, it gives one clean pulse each time it's triggered. Crystals provide the accurate clocks inside every microcontroller.","ideas":["In astable mode, a 555 charges its capacitor through R1 + R2 and discharges it through R2 only.","That makes the high time always longer than the low time, so the duty cycle is above 50%.","The capacitor swings between one third and two thirds of the supply voltage.","A diode across R2 bypasses it while charging, which allows duty cycles below 50%.","Monostable mode gives a single pulse lasting about 1.1 × R × C.","Above about 25 Hz, a blinking LED looks steady to your eye. Its brightness follows the duty cycle.","Quartz crystals vibrate at a very stable mechanical frequency, accurate to tens of parts per million."],"formulas":[["f = 1.44 / ((R1 + 2R2) · C)","555 astable frequency"],["tH = 0.693 · (R1 + R2) · C","555 high time"],["tL = 0.693 · R2 · C","555 low time"],["D = (R1 + R2) / (R1 + 2R2)","555 duty cycle"],["t = 1.1 · R · C","555 monostable pulse"]],"labs":["timer555"],"quiz":[{"q":"A 555 astable has R1 = 1 kΩ, R2 = 10 kΩ and C = 10 µF. Its frequency is about:","o":["0.7 Hz","6.9 Hz","69 Hz","1.4 kHz"],"a":1,"w":"f = 1.44 / ((1k + 20k) × 10 µF) = 1.44 / 0.21 ≈ 6.9 Hz."},{"q":"In the standard 555 astable circuit, the duty cycle is:","o":["Always below 50%","Always above 50%","Exactly 50%","Set only by C"],"a":1,"w":"The capacitor charges through R1 + R2 but discharges through R2 alone, so the high time is always longer."},{"q":"A 555 monostable with R = 100 kΩ and C = 10 µF gives a pulse of about:","o":["0.11 s","1.1 s","11 s","1 ms"],"a":1,"w":"1.1 × 100,000 × 0.00001 = 1.1 s."},{"q":"Which oscillator is used for accurate microcontroller clocks?","o":["RC relaxation","555 astable","Quartz crystal","Wien bridge"],"a":2,"w":"Quartz crystals resonate at a very stable frequency."},{"q":"A 555's capacitor voltage swings between:","o":["0 and Vcc","⅓ and ⅔ of Vcc","½ Vcc and Vcc","0 and 0.7 V"],"a":1,"w":"The internal comparators trip at one third and two thirds of the supply voltage."}],"reading":"pp. 683–698","part":"B","ex":[{"q":"Design a 555 astable that blinks an LED at about 2 Hz with roughly 50% duty cycle. Use C = 10 µF and R1 = 1 kΩ. Choose R2 and check the result.","steps":["The frequency formula gives R1 + 2·R2 = 1.44 / (f · C) = 1.44 / (2 Hz × 10 µF) = 72 kΩ.","R2 = (72 kΩ − 1 kΩ) / 2 = 35.5 kΩ. Take the E24 value 36 kΩ.","Check: R1 + 2·R2 = 1 kΩ + 72 kΩ = 73 kΩ, so f = 1.44 / (73 kΩ × 10 µF) = 1.97 Hz.","High time: tH = 0.693 × (1 kΩ + 36 kΩ) × 10 µF = 256 ms. Low time: tL = 0.693 × 36 kΩ × 10 µF = 249 ms. Total 505 ms.","Duty cycle: (R1 + R2) / (R1 + 2·R2) = 37 kΩ / 73 kΩ = 50.7%. Making R1 small compared with R2 is what pushes the duty cycle toward 50%. The electrolytic capacitor's ±20% tolerance will move the frequency by about that much."],"ans":"R2 = 36 kΩ gives 1.97 Hz and 50.7% duty cycle (high 256 ms, low 249 ms)."},{"q":"A 555 monostable must hold a relay on for 3 s after each trigger. Choose R with C = 10 µF and estimate the spread caused by the capacitor's tolerance.","steps":["t = 1.1 · R · C, so R = t / (1.1 × C) = 3 s / (1.1 × 10 µF) = 273 kΩ.","Standard values: E12 has 270 kΩ. Check: t = 1.1 × 270 kΩ × 10 µF = 2.97 s, which is 1% short.","If the capacitor is ±20%, the pulse is 2.97 s × 0.8 = 2.38 s at worst low and 2.97 s × 1.2 = 3.56 s at worst high, ignoring the resistor's ±5%.","If 3 s must hold within a few percent, use a film capacitor, which has a tight tolerance and little drift, or a microcontroller timer."],"ans":"270 kΩ gives 2.97 s, which can range from 2.38 s to 3.56 s with a ±20% electrolytic."}],"deep":[{"t":"Deriving the 555 astable formula","body":"Inside the 555, three equal 5 kΩ resistors form a divider that sets the comparator thresholds at ⅓ and ⅔ of the supply Vcc. The timing capacitor swings between these two voltages, regardless of Vcc.\n\nCharging goes through R1 + R2 toward Vcc, from Vcc/3 up to 2Vcc/3. The capacitor voltage follows v = Vcc − (Vcc − Vcc/3) · e^(−t/τ) with τ = (R1 + R2) · C. At the end, v = 2Vcc/3:\n\n= Vcc − (2Vcc/3) · e^(−t/τ) = 2Vcc/3\n= e^(−t/τ) = (Vcc/3) / (2Vcc/3) = ½\n= tH = τ · ln 2 = 0.693 · (R1 + R2) · C\n\nDischarging goes through R2 only (the discharge pin shorts it to ground), from 2Vcc/3 down to Vcc/3. Here v = (2Vcc/3) · e^(−t/τ) with τ = R2 · C, and the same ratio of ½ appears:\n\n= tL = 0.693 · R2 · C\n\nAdding them, T = 0.693 · (R1 + 2·R2) · C, so f = 1 / T = 1.443 / ((R1 + 2·R2) · C), the 1.44 in the formula sheet. Vcc cancels, which is why the 555 frequency barely changes with supply voltage.\n\nMonostable timing charges from 0 to 2Vcc/3 through R, so e^(−t/τ) = ⅓ and t = τ · ln 3 = 1.0986 · R · C, rounded to 1.1 · R · C.\n\nPractical limit: R1 should not be too small, since the discharge pin connects it across Vcc while it discharges. Keep it at 1 kΩ or more on 12 V."},{"t":"Duty cycle limits and the diode trick","body":"In the standard astable, D = (R1 + R2) / (R1 + 2·R2). That is always above 50%, since the capacitor charges through R1 + R2 but discharges through R2 alone. When R2 is much larger than R1, D approaches 50% from above. When R1 is much larger than R2, D approaches 100%. For R1 = 100 kΩ and R2 = 1 kΩ it is 101/102 = 99%.\n\nTo get below 50%, add a diode across R2 with its anode on the discharge-pin side and its cathode on the capacitor side. While charging, current goes from R1 through the diode and bypasses R2. While discharging, the diode is reverse-biased and the current goes through R2 as before:\n\n= tH ≈ 0.693 · R1 · C\n= tL = 0.693 · R2 · C\n= D = R1 / (R1 + R2)\n\nExample: R1 = 1 kΩ, R2 = 9 kΩ, C = 1 µF gives tH = 0.69 ms, tL = 6.2 ms, T = 6.9 ms, f = 144 Hz and D = 10%.\n\nA real diode drops about 0.6 V, so the capacitor charges toward Vcc − 0.6 V, not toward Vcc. This lengthens tH. For a 5 V supply the charge from ⅓ to ⅔ takes about 0.94 · R1 · C instead of 0.69, 36% longer. On 12 V it takes about 0.78 · R1 · C, 12% longer. Calculate with this figure, or adjust R1 by trial.\n\nFor exact control of duty cycle and frequency, a microcontroller's PWM output is the better tool."},{"t":"Crystal versus RC accuracy","body":"An RC oscillator's frequency depends on the absolute values of R and C, and on switching thresholds. A 1% resistor with a 5% film capacitor gives about ±6% at room temperature. A ±20% electrolytic gives ±20%. Add temperature coefficients and ageing, and 10% or more is normal. The internal RC oscillator of a microcontroller does a little better, around ±1 to 2% after factory trimming.\n\nA quartz crystal is a mechanical resonator, and its frequency depends on the cut and size of the crystal, so it is very stable. Ordinary parts are accurate to about ±10 to ±30 ppm (parts per million). The effect on a clock, using 20 ppm:\n\n- Error per day: 86,400 s × 20 × 10⁻⁶ = 1.7 s\n- Error per 30 days: about 52 s\n- A ±5% RC clock would drift 86,400 s × 0.05 = 4320 s a day, 72 minutes\n\nA crystal needs load capacitors to oscillate at its labelled frequency. For two equal capacitors C and stray capacitance Cs, the load seen is:\n\n= C_L = C / 2 + Cs\n\nSo C = 2 · (C_L − Cs). With C_L = 18 pF and Cs = 4 pF, C = 28 pF, so use 27 pF. Wrong values pull the frequency off, or stop it starting.\n\nThe 32.768 kHz watch crystal is popular because 32,768 = 2¹⁵: a chain of 15 divide-by-two stages gives exactly 1 Hz. The cost of a crystal is a slower start, up to milliseconds for MHz types and a second or so for a watch crystal."}]},
{"n":11,"title":"Digital Logic","short":"Digital","blurb":"Binary, logic gates, adders, flip-flops, counters, logic levels and FPGAs.","big":"Digital circuits treat every voltage as a 1 or a 0. From a handful of gates you can build adders, memory and counters, and eventually whole processors. Microcontrollers now do most of what discrete logic chips used to, but you still need gates, binary, flip-flops and logic levels to connect anything to anything. FPGAs take this further by letting you configure custom hardware.","ideas":["Binary and hexadecimal are different ways to write the same numbers. One hex digit is exactly four bits.","The basic gates are AND, OR, NOT, NAND, NOR and XOR. NAND alone, or NOR alone, can build any logic function.","A half adder is an XOR for the sum and an AND for the carry. Chain full adders to add numbers of any width.","Combinational logic has no memory. Sequential logic, built from flip-flops, stores state and updates on clock edges.","A binary counter divides frequency. Each stage toggles at half the rate of the one before it.","Shift registers turn serial data into parallel outputs, which lets you drive many LEDs from three pins.","Logic families differ in voltage thresholds. Use a level shifter between 5 V and 3.3 V logic.","Never leave CMOS inputs floating. Tie unused inputs high or low.","FPGAs contain configurable logic that all runs in parallel, programmed in a hardware description language like Verilog or VHDL."],"formulas":[["¬(A · B) = ¬A + ¬B","De Morgan's law"],["S = A ⊕ B, C = A · B","Half adder"],["f_out = f_in / 2ⁿ","n-stage binary counter"],["2ⁿ values from n bits","Range of an n-bit number"]],"labs":["logic","adder","bits"],"quiz":[{"q":"0x2F in decimal is:","o":["32","47","215","29"],"a":1,"w":"2 × 16 + 15 = 47."},{"q":"Which gate outputs 1 only when its inputs differ?","o":["AND","OR","XOR","NAND"],"a":2,"w":"Exclusive OR is true when exactly one input is true."},{"q":"Why tie unused CMOS inputs to a fixed level?","o":["To make the chip faster","Floating inputs pick up noise and can cause erratic behavior and extra current","They need power","To set the clock"],"a":1,"w":"A floating input can drift to the middle of its range and switch randomly."},{"q":"A half adder gets A = 1 and B = 1. It outputs:","o":["Sum 1, carry 0","Sum 0, carry 1","Sum 1, carry 1","Sum 0, carry 0"],"a":1,"w":"1 + 1 = 10 in binary: the sum bit is 0 and the carry is 1."},{"q":"An 8-bit unsigned number can hold values from 0 to:","o":["127","255","256","1023"],"a":1,"w":"2⁸ = 256 values, so 0 to 255."},{"q":"A 1 MHz clock feeds a 4-stage binary counter. The last stage toggles at:","o":["250 kHz","125 kHz","62.5 kHz","4 MHz"],"a":2,"w":"1 MHz / 2⁴ = 62.5 kHz."}],"reading":"pp. 717–842, 897–932","part":"B","ex":[{"q":"Add 0xB7 and 0x4F in an 8-bit adder. Give the 8-bit result and the carry out, then read both operands and the result as two's-complement numbers.","steps":["Binary: 0xB7 = 1011 0111 and 0x4F = 0100 1111.","Low nibble: 0x7 + 0xF = 7 + 15 = 22 = 0x16. The sum digit is 6 and the carry into the high nibble is 1.","High nibble: 0xB + 0x4 + 1 = 11 + 4 + 1 = 16 = 0x10. The sum digit is 0 and the carry out of bit 7 is 1.","Result: 0x06 with carry out 1. As unsigned numbers that is 183 + 79 = 262 = 256 + 6, so the carry holds the missing 256.","As signed numbers: 0xB7 = 183 − 256 = −73 and 0x4F = +79, so the sum is −73 + 79 = +6, which matches 0x06. The operands have opposite signs, so the signed result cannot overflow and the carry out can be ignored."],"ans":"0x06 with carry 1. Unsigned: 183 + 79 = 262. Signed: −73 + 79 = +6."},{"q":"A 3.3 V microcontroller pin (guaranteed VOH ≥ 2.9 V, VOL ≤ 0.4 V) must drive a 5 V logic input. Compare a 74HC part (VIH ≥ 3.5 V, VIL ≤ 1.5 V) with a 74HCT part (VIH ≥ 2.0 V, VIL ≤ 0.8 V). Then check the return path from a 5 V HCT output (VOH ≥ 4.4 V).","steps":["Noise margin high: NMH = VOH − VIH. For 74HC: 2.9 V − 3.5 V = −0.6 V. It is negative, so a 3.3 V high is not guaranteed to be read as high.","For 74HCT: NMH = 2.9 V − 2.0 V = 0.9 V. Noise margin low: NML = VIL − VOL = 0.8 V − 0.4 V = 0.4 V. Both are positive, so HCT works. (For HC, NML = 1.5 V − 0.4 V = 1.1 V is fine, but the high side already fails.)","Return path: a 5 V output of 4.4 V or more must not reach a 3.3 V pin that tolerates at most about 3.6 V (Vcc + 0.3 V). Use a divider with 1 kΩ on top and 2 kΩ to ground.","Divider output: 4.4 V × 2 kΩ / (1 kΩ + 2 kΩ) = 2.93 V. The full 5 V gives 3.33 V. The microcontroller reads a high above 0.7 × 3.3 V = 2.31 V, so both are valid highs and neither exceeds the pin's limit.","The divider's 667 Ω source impedance (1 kΩ ‖ 2 kΩ) with a 10 pF input gives about 6.7 ns, fast enough for logic signals up to a few MHz. For faster signals use a level-shifter chip."],"ans":"74HC fails (NMH = −0.6 V). 74HCT works (NMH = 0.9 V, NML = 0.4 V). A 1 kΩ / 2 kΩ divider returns 2.93 V to 3.3 V logic."}],"deep":[{"t":"Logic levels and noise margins","body":"A digital input has two thresholds. Below VIL it must read 0, above VIH it must read 1, and in between the result is undefined. An output guarantees VOL at most and VOH at least. The gap between what a driver guarantees and what a receiver needs is the noise margin:\n\n= NMH = VOH − VIH\n= NML = VIL − VOL\n\nThe margin is what crosstalk, ground bounce and supply ripple must stay under. A 3.3 V LVTTL output (VOH 2.4 V, VOL 0.4 V) into a 3.3 V LVTTL input (VIH 2.0 V, VIL 0.8 V) has 0.4 V of margin on each side. A 74LS output (2.7 V, 0.5 V) into a 74LS input has 0.7 V high and 0.3 V low.\n\nCMOS thresholds scale with the supply, about 0.7 × Vcc and 0.3 × Vcc. At 5 V that is 3.5 V and 1.5 V. At 3.3 V it is 2.31 V and 0.99 V. TTL-compatible inputs (the T in 74HCT) are fixed near 2.0 V and 0.8 V.\n\nSo a 3.3 V output can drive a 5 V HCT input, but not a 5 V HC input. A 5 V output into a 3.3 V part is a different problem: the level is valid, but the input protection diodes clamp at about Vcc + 0.5 V, and current into the clamp can damage the part. Three ways around it:\n\n- Use a part whose inputs tolerate 5 V (many 74LVC parts and some microcontroller pins say so in the datasheet)\n- A resistor divider, for slow signals\n- A level-shifter chip, or a MOSFET shifter for open-drain buses like I²C\n\nThresholds in datasheets depend on supply, temperature and load, so read the table for your supply voltage."},{"t":"Propagation delay and glitches","body":"Gates are not instant. The propagation delay tpd, typically 5 to 15 ns for 74HC at 5 V, is the time from an input crossing its threshold to the output crossing its own. It differs between paths, and that causes glitches.\n\nExample: Y = A · B + (NOT A) · C with B = C = 1. Y should stay 1 whatever A does. When A falls, the term A · B drops at once, but (NOT A) · C waits for the inverter, say 8 ns, before it rises. For those 8 ns both terms are 0 and Y dips low. A latch or counter listening to Y can catch that dip. Adding the redundant term B · C holds Y high through the change.\n\nA ripple counter shows the same effect. With 12 ns per flip-flop, the count 0111 → 1000 passes through 0110, 0100 and 0000 before it settles at 1000, over 48 ns. A decoder watching that counter will fire on the false states.\n\nThe fix is synchronous design: let every flip-flop change on one clock edge, and only look at the outputs after they settle. The clock period must cover all the delays in the slowest path:\n\n= T_min = t_cq + t_logic + t_setup\n\nWith t_cq = 10 ns, 25 ns of logic and t_setup = 5 ns, T_min = 40 ns, so the clock can run at up to 25 MHz. Hold time is the reverse worry: data must not change too soon after the edge. A very short path can violate it even at slow clock speeds."},{"t":"Open-drain outputs and the wired-AND","body":"A push-pull output drives the line both high and low. An open-drain output (open-collector on bipolar parts) only pulls low. To get a high, an external pull-up resistor takes the line up to its supply.\n\nThat has two useful consequences. Several open-drain outputs can share one line. The line is high only when every output is off, so it is the AND of the outputs, called a wired-AND. In active-low terms, any device can pull the line low to say 'I need attention', which is how shared interrupt lines and I²C work. And the pull-up can go to a different voltage than the chip's supply, so one open-drain pin can talk to 5 V logic through a 5 V pull-up, if the pin tolerates it.\n\nTwo push-pull outputs fighting over a line do damage. If one drives high and the other low, each with about 50 Ω of output resistance, the current is 5 V / (50 Ω + 50 Ω) = 50 mA, twice the 25 mA rating of a 74HC pin.\n\nThe pull-up sets a trade-off:\n\n= R_min = (Vcc − VOL) / IOL\n\nWith 3.3 V and 3 mA sink current, R_min = (3.3 V − 0.4 V) / 3 mA = 967 Ω. At 5 V it is 1.53 kΩ. Larger R saves power (a 10 kΩ pull-up wastes 0.33 mA while the line is held low at 3.3 V) but makes the rising edge slower, since it is an RC charge. Open-drain edges are fast falling and slow rising."}]},
{"n":12,"title":"Microcontrollers and Sensors","short":"MCUs","blurb":"GPIO, ADCs, PWM, communication buses, sensors and breakout modules.","big":"A microcontroller is a tiny computer with memory and input/output pins, and it's the brain of most modern gadgets. This module covers how its pins work, how it reads the analog world through an ADC, how it fakes analog output with PWM, and how it talks to sensors and modules over I²C, SPI and UART. It also covers reading resistive sensors such as thermistors.","ideas":["A microcontroller combines a CPU, flash memory for the program, RAM for data, and peripherals: GPIO, ADC, timers, PWM, UART, I²C and SPI.","A GPIO pin can source or sink only a few to about 20 milliamps, depending on the chip. Use a transistor or MOSFET for anything bigger than an LED.","Read a pushbutton with a pull-up or pull-down resistor (often built into the chip), and debounce it in software.","An n-bit ADC splits its reference voltage into 2ⁿ steps.","PWM switches a pin on and off quickly. The average voltage equals the duty cycle times the supply.","I²C shares two wires among many addressed devices. SPI is faster and uses a chip-select line per device. UART is simple point-to-point serial.","A 5 V signal can damage a 3.3 V input. Check logic levels before connecting anything.","Resistive sensors such as thermistors, photoresistors and force sensors are read through a voltage divider into an ADC pin.","Before designing a subsystem from scratch, check whether a breakout board or module already does it."],"formulas":[["LSB = Vref / 2ⁿ","ADC step size"],["code ≈ Vin / Vref × 2ⁿ","ADC reading"],["Vavg = D · Vcc","PWM average voltage"],["R(T) = R₀ · e^(B(1/T − 1/T₀))","NTC thermistor (T in kelvin)"]],"labs":["adc","pwm","ntc"],"quiz":[{"q":"What can an Arduino GPIO pin drive directly?","o":["A 12 V motor","A single LED with a resistor","A relay coil drawing 80 mA","A speaker at full volume"],"a":1,"w":"A classic Arduino pin can supply about 20 mA safely, and many 3.3 V chips allow less. Anything more needs a driver transistor."},{"q":"Which bus uses just two wires (SDA and SCL) shared by many devices?","o":["SPI","UART","I²C","USB"],"a":2,"w":"I²C devices share a data line and a clock line and are selected by address."},{"q":"A 5 V PWM signal at 30% duty cycle averages:","o":["1.5 V","3.5 V","0.3 V","5 V"],"a":0,"w":"0.30 × 5 = 1.5 V."},{"q":"A 10-bit ADC with a 3.3 V reference has a step size of about:","o":["3.2 mV","33 mV","0.33 V","1 mV"],"a":0,"w":"3.3 / 1024 ≈ 3.2 mV per step."},{"q":"As an NTC thermistor heats up, its resistance:","o":["Rises","Falls","Stays constant","Becomes negative"],"a":1,"w":"NTC means negative temperature coefficient: resistance falls as temperature rises."},{"q":"Why debounce a pushbutton?","o":["To make it last longer","Its contacts bounce, producing several transitions per press","To lower its resistance","To save power"],"a":1,"w":"Mechanical contacts bounce for a few milliseconds. Without debouncing, one press can register as several."}],"reading":"pp. 843–896, 525–550, 963–972","part":"B","ex":[{"q":"A 10 kΩ NTC thermistor (B = 3950, 10 kΩ at 25 °C) sits on top of a divider with a fixed 10 kΩ resistor to ground. A 10-bit ADC with a 3.3 V reference reads it. Find the reading at 50 °C and the size of one step in °C.","steps":["Convert to kelvin: 50 °C = 323.15 K and 25 °C = 298.15 K.","R(50 °C) = 10 kΩ × e^(3950 × (1/323.15 K − 1/298.15 K)) = 10 kΩ × e^(−1.0249) = 3.59 kΩ.","Divider: Vout = 3.3 V × 10 kΩ / (3.59 kΩ + 10 kΩ) = 3.3 V × 0.7359 = 2.43 V.","ADC code ≈ Vout / Vref × 1024 = 2.43 V / 3.3 V × 1024 = 753.","One step: LSB = 3.3 V / 1024 = 3.22 mV. At 51 °C the output is 2.45 V, a change of about 24 mV per °C, so one step is 3.22 mV / 24 mV/°C = 0.13 °C.","At 25 °C both resistors are 10 kΩ and the output sits at 1.65 V, code 512. The slope is steepest near that midpoint, which is why the fixed resistor is chosen to match the thermistor's value at the temperature you care about."],"ans":"R = 3.59 kΩ, Vout = 2.43 V, code 753. One step is 3.22 mV, about 0.13 °C."},{"q":"An 8-bit PWM on a 16 MHz timer with a prescaler of 64 drives an LED on a 5 V pin with analogWrite(100). Find the PWM frequency, duty cycle and average voltage. Then find the ripple if a 10 kΩ and 1 µF RC filter turns it into a DC level.","steps":["Frequency: f = 16 MHz / (64 × 256) = 976.6 Hz, a period of 1.024 ms. That is fast enough to look steady to the eye.","Duty cycle: analogWrite takes 0 to 255, so D ≈ 100 / 255 = 39.2%. The exact count depends on the chip, within about 0.3%.","Average voltage: Vavg = D × Vcc = 0.392 × 5 V = 1.96 V.","Filter: τ = 10 kΩ × 1 µF = 10 ms, and the filter's cutoff is 1 / (2π × 10 ms) = 15.9 Hz, about 61 times below the PWM frequency.","Triangular ripple, when τ is much longer than the period: ΔV ≈ Vcc · D · (1 − D) / (f · τ) = 5 V × 0.392 × 0.608 / (976.6 Hz × 10 ms) = 122 mV peak to peak.","One 8-bit step of the PWM is 5 V / 255 = 19.6 mV, so the ripple spans about 6 steps. For a quieter output use a bigger RC product or a second filter stage."],"ans":"976.6 Hz, 39.2% duty, 1.96 V average. The 10 kΩ and 1 µF filter leaves about 122 mV of ripple (about 6 steps of 19.6 mV)."}],"deep":[{"t":"Quantisation and why the reference matters","body":"An n-bit ADC cuts its range into 2ⁿ steps, so the smallest change it can see is:\n\n= LSB = Vref / 2ⁿ\n\nEvery reading is rounded to a step, an error of up to ±½ LSB. For an ideal converter fed a full-scale sine wave, that limits the signal-to-noise ratio:\n\n= SNR ≈ 6.02 · n + 1.76 dB\n\nFor 10 bits this is 62 dB, for 12 bits 74 dB. Real converters fall short by a bit or two (the effective number of bits, ENOB) because of their own noise.\n\nThe ADC compares your signal to its reference, so any error in the reference is an error in every reading. A 10-bit ADC on a 3.3 V supply used as the reference has 3.22 mV steps. If that supply is ±1% (33 mV), the gain error is 10 steps at full scale. On a 12-bit ADC (0.806 mV steps) the same 1% is 41 steps, so the two extra bits only add resolution, not accuracy. An internal bandgap reference (about 1.1 V) is often within a few percent, and a dedicated reference chip is within 0.1% or better.\n\nWhen the sensor needs no absolute value, avoid the problem with a ratiometric measurement. Power the thermistor divider from the same Vref the ADC uses. The reading becomes Rf / (Rt + Rf) × 2ⁿ, and Vref cancels. This is why potentiometers, thermistors and bridge sensors are usually fed from the ADC reference."},{"t":"I²C pull-ups and rise time","body":"I²C lines are open-drain, so each line rises through a pull-up resistor R into the bus capacitance C (pin capacitance, wire and connectors, often 10 to 400 pF). The rising edge is an RC charge. The standard measure is the time from 30% to 70% of Vcc:\n\n= t_r = R · C · ln(0.7 / 0.3) ≈ 0.85 · R · C\n\nThe I²C specification allows t_r up to 1000 ns at 100 kHz (standard mode) and 300 ns at 400 kHz (fast mode). That sets a ceiling on R. The sink current of the devices sets a floor:\n\n= R_min = (Vcc − 0.4 V) / 3 mA\n\nExample: 400 pF of bus capacitance on 3.3 V.\n\n- Standard mode: R ≤ 1000 ns / (0.85 × 400 pF) = 2.9 kΩ, and R ≥ 967 Ω, so choose about 2.2 kΩ\n- Fast mode: R ≤ 300 ns / (0.85 × 400 pF) = 880 Ω, which is below the 967 Ω floor, so no value works\n\nIn that case reduce the bus capacitance (shorter wires, fewer devices), slow the bus down, or use a bus buffer. Most small boards (30 to 50 pF) are comfortable with 4.7 kΩ at 100 kHz: t_r = 0.85 × 4.7 kΩ × 50 pF = 200 ns. Many breakout boards already carry pull-ups, 4.7 kΩ to 10 kΩ each. Put four of them on one bus and they are in parallel, 1.2 kΩ to 2.5 kΩ, so the total may be lower than you want."},{"t":"Debouncing: an RC filter or software","body":"A mechanical switch does not close cleanly. The contacts bounce for 1 to 10 ms, producing a burst of edges. A microcontroller sampling at MHz speeds sees each one.\n\nSoftware debouncing costs no parts. Two common methods:\n\n- Sample the pin every 1 to 5 ms and accept a new state only after it has been the same for a set number of samples, such as 5 samples at 2 ms (10 ms)\n- On the first edge, start a timer and ignore the pin until it expires, such as 20 ms\n\nBoth cost a little code and some latency, which for a human press is invisible. They are also easy to adjust once you see the real switch.\n\nHardware debouncing uses an RC filter. With a 10 kΩ pull-up, a 47 kΩ resistor and a 100 nF capacitor across the switch work like this: closing the switch discharges the capacitor almost instantly, and opening it lets the capacitor charge through the resistor. Time to reach the 70% input threshold is R · C · ln(1/0.3) = 1.2 · R · C. With 47 kΩ and 100 nF, τ = 4.7 ms and the delay is 5.7 ms, so a short burst of bounces never lets the voltage rise to the threshold. A slowly rising edge needs a Schmitt-trigger input, or the input hovers in the undefined zone and draws extra current. Most microcontroller inputs have one, but check.\n\nUse the RC filter when the signal drives an interrupt, where each bounce would cost a call to the handler, or when there is no firmware to adjust. Otherwise software is cheaper. Switches with sparks, such as relay contacts, are much worse than a tact switch, so allow more time."}]},
{"n":13,"title":"Motors","short":"Motors","blurb":"DC motors, H-bridges, servos and stepper motors.","big":"Three motor types cover most projects. Brushed DC motors are controlled with PWM for speed and an H-bridge for direction. Hobby servos take a pulse whose width sets their angle. Steppers move in precise steps, driven by a dedicated chip. Motors draw large, spiky currents, so they always need a driver and usually their own supply.","ideas":["DC motor speed follows the average voltage, which you set with PWM through a MOSFET.","An H-bridge is four switches arranged so current can flow either way through the motor, which lets it reverse.","Always put a flyback diode across a motor or other coil switched by a single transistor.","Hobby servos expect a pulse about every 20 ms. Around 1 ms is one end of travel, 1.5 ms is center, and 2 ms is the other end.","Stepper motors are unipolar (5–6 wires) or bipolar (4 wires). Driver chips handle the coil sequence and microstepping.","Full stepping energizes two coils at a time for more torque. Half stepping alternates between one and two coils for finer steps.","Motor stall current can be 5–10 times the running current. Size the supply and the driver for it.","Share a ground between the motor supply and the logic, but route the motor current so it doesn't flow through the logic's ground wires."],"formulas":[["Vavg = D · Vsupply","PWM motor voltage"],["steps/rev = 360° / step angle","Stepper resolution"],["angle ≈ (pulse − 1 ms) × 180°/ms","Typical servo mapping"]],"labs":["servo","stepper"],"quiz":[{"q":"To reverse a brushed DC motor electronically, you use:","o":["A single MOSFET","An H-bridge","A Zener diode","A crystal"],"a":1,"w":"An H-bridge can apply the supply across the motor in either polarity."},{"q":"A hobby servo at its center position receives a pulse of about:","o":["0.5 ms","1.5 ms","5 ms","20 ms"],"a":1,"w":"About 1–2 ms covers the travel, and 1.5 ms is center. Pulses repeat about every 20 ms."},{"q":"A stepper with a 1.8° step angle has how many full steps per revolution?","o":["100","180","200","360"],"a":2,"w":"360 / 1.8 = 200."},{"q":"In half-step mode, that same motor takes how many steps per revolution?","o":["100","200","400","800"],"a":2,"w":"Half stepping doubles the count: 400 steps of 0.9°."},{"q":"A motor runs at 0.5 A but stalls at 4 A. The driver should be rated for at least:","o":["0.5 A","1 A","4 A","2 A"],"a":2,"w":"Motors draw stall current at every start and whenever they jam. Size for the worst case."}],"reading":"pp. 933–946","part":"B","ex":[{"q":"A 12 V brushed DC motor has a winding resistance of 3 Ω and runs at 0.8 A. Find its stall current and the back-EMF while running, then size a MOSFET driver and the power supply.","steps":["At stall the shaft does not turn, so there is no back-EMF. Only the winding resistance limits the current: I_stall = V / R = 12 V / 3 Ω = 4 A, five times the running current.","While running, the back-EMF opposes the supply: E = V − I · R = 12 V − 0.8 A × 3 Ω = 9.6 V.","Design for the worst case. The driver and the wiring must handle 4 A, so a MOSFET rated for 6 A or more is a sensible start. With Rds(on) = 20 mΩ it dissipates 4² × 0.02 = 0.32 W at stall and 0.8² × 0.02 = 12.8 mW when running.","The supply must deliver 12 V × 4 A = 48 W at stall, even though the motor draws only 12 V × 0.8 A = 9.6 W normally. Use a supply rated for that, or add a current limit.","Stalling is also a heat problem for the motor. All 12 V × 4 A = 48 W goes to the winding, compared with 0.8² × 3 = 1.92 W of winding loss when running. A small motor will burn in seconds, so a current limit or a stall timeout is part of the design."],"ans":"Stall current 4 A, running back-EMF 9.6 V. The driver and supply must handle 4 A (48 W). Stall dissipates 48 W in the winding."},{"q":"A NEMA 17 stepper (1.8° per full step) on a 1/16-microstep driver turns an 8 mm-lead screw at 120 rpm. Find the step rate, the time between step pulses and the linear resolution and speed.","steps":["Steps per revolution: (360° / 1.8°) × 16 = 200 × 16 = 3200 microsteps.","Revolutions per second: 120 rpm / 60 s = 2 rev/s.","Step rate: 3200 steps/rev × 2 rev/s = 6400 steps/s, so the time between pulses is 1 / 6400 Hz = 156 µs.","Linear resolution: 8 mm / 3200 = 2.5 µm per microstep. Linear speed: 8 mm × 2 rev/s = 16 mm/s.","A driver chip can accept pulses that short (typical minimum pulse width is 1 µs), but whether the motor can follow is a torque question. The coil current needs time to build up, so a motor at 6400 microsteps/s has less torque than one at 100 steps/s. Check the torque-speed curve before trusting these figures."],"ans":"6400 steps/s, 156 µs between pulses. Resolution 2.5 µm, speed 16 mm/s."}],"deep":[{"t":"Back-EMF and why stall current is the design current","body":"A spinning motor is also a generator. Its winding moves through a magnetic field and produces a back-EMF proportional to speed:\n\n= E = k_e · ω\n= I = (V − E) / R\n\nA motor reaches its running speed when E has grown until the net current gives just enough torque for the load. In SI units the torque constant and the back-EMF constant are the same number: T = k_t · I, with k_t = k_e in N·m/A and V·s/rad.\n\nExample: the 12 V, 3 Ω motor with k_e = 0.02 V·s/rad. At 0.8 A, E = 9.6 V and ω = 9.6 V / 0.02 = 480 rad/s = 4580 rpm. Torque is 0.02 × 0.8 A = 16 mN·m, mechanical power is E · I = 9.6 V × 0.8 A = 7.68 W and efficiency is 7.68 / 9.6 = 80%. At stall the current is 4 A and the torque is 80 mN·m, five times higher.\n\nTwo consequences for the design:\n\n- The worst-case current is at standstill, so size fuses, drivers and supplies for stall (V / R), not for the running current\n- Reversing a spinning motor by flipping the bridge is worse than stalling. The back-EMF now adds to the supply: I = (V + E) / R = (12 V + 9.6 V) / 3 Ω = 7.2 A. Ramp the PWM down and brake first\n\nPWM works for speed because the average voltage sets the speed at which E balances it, and the inductance smooths the current between pulses. Lowering the duty cycle lowers the speed, and the torque stays available at any speed."},{"t":"H-bridge shoot-through and dead time","body":"An H-bridge has two half-bridges, each a high-side and a low-side switch in series across the supply. If both switches in one half-bridge are on at once, nothing limits the current but the switches and the wiring. That is called shoot-through. With 20 mΩ MOSFETs on 12 V, the current is 12 V / (2 × 0.02 Ω) = 300 A until something burns.\n\nIt happens because switches turn off more slowly than they turn on. A MOSFET gate must discharge before the channel closes, which takes tens to hundreds of nanoseconds. If the other switch gets its turn-on command at the same moment, both conduct for a short time.\n\nThe fix is dead time: after commanding a switch off, wait before turning its partner on. Choose it longer than the slowest turn-off plus a safety margin, such as 200 to 500 ns for typical power MOSFETs. During dead time the motor current continues through a body diode (or a flyback diode) of the opposite switch, so it is not interrupted.\n\nDead time costs a little control. At a 20 kHz PWM, the period is 50 µs. Two dead times of 0.5 µs per period take 1 µs, or 2% of the period, from the commanded duty.\n\nIn practice:\n\n- Use a gate-driver chip or an integrated motor driver, which includes dead time and shoot-through protection\n- Microcontroller timers for motor control have a hardware dead-time register\n- Never drive the two halves from separate GPIO writes in software without it\n- N-channel high-side switches need a gate voltage above the supply, so use a driver with a bootstrap capacitor"},{"t":"Stepper torque against speed, and microstepping","body":"A stepper coil is an inductor with resistance, so the current rises with a time constant of L / R. A typical NEMA 17 motor has 2.8 Ω and 4 mH, so τ = 4 mH / 2.8 Ω = 1.43 ms. At 6400 microsteps/s, a microstep lasts only 156 µs, so the current never reaches its set value. Torque falls as speed rises, and above a limit the motor skips steps or stalls.\n\nTwo ways to hold torque at speed:\n\n- A higher supply voltage. Chopper drivers (such as the A4988 and DRV8825) switch the coil voltage to hold a current limit. They can run from 24 V on a motor rated 4 V, which makes the current rise about 6 times faster\n- Acceleration ramps, which let the rotor follow instead of lagging behind. Starting at full speed from rest usually fails\n\nA stepper holds its position only while powered and loaded below its holding torque. If the load pulls it past that, steps are lost, and with no feedback, the controller cannot know.\n\nMicrostepping drives the two coils with sine and cosine currents instead of full on or off. At 1/16, a 1.8° step becomes 16 positions of 0.1125° each. It smooths motion and reduces resonance and noise, but does not give the same gain in accuracy. The restoring torque follows the sine of the angle error, so near a position it is weak. With a load of half the holding torque, the shaft lags by asin(0.5) = 30° electrical, which is 0.6° mechanical, about 5 microsteps. Datasheets promise about ±5% of a full step (±0.09°) between full-step positions. Use fine microstepping for smoothness, not extra accuracy."}]},
{"n":14,"title":"AC Signals and Decibels","short":"AC · dB","blurb":"Sine waves, RMS, reactance, impedance and the decibel scale.","big":"Mains power, audio and radio all use alternating signals. A sine wave is described by its amplitude, frequency and phase. RMS is the value that delivers the same heating power as DC, so it's the number quoted for mains and audio. Capacitors and inductors respond differently at different frequencies, and decibels let you compare ratios that span millions to one.","ideas":["A sine wave is the shadow of a rotating arrow, called a phasor. One full turn is one cycle.","Frequency (Hz) is the number of cycles per second, and the period T = 1 / f.","For a sine wave, RMS = peak / √2 ≈ 0.707 × peak. 120 V RMS mains peaks at about 170 V. 230 V mains peaks at about 325 V.","A capacitor's opposition to AC (its reactance) falls as frequency rises. An inductor's rises.","Impedance (Z) combines resistance and reactance and is measured in ohms.","Decibels compress huge ratios. +20 dB is 10× the voltage, +6 dB is about 2× the voltage, and +3 dB is about 2× the power.","Input and output impedance decide how much of a signal survives the trip from one stage to the next."],"formulas":[["Vrms = Vpeak / √2","Sine wave RMS"],["T = 1 / f","Period"],["Xc = 1 / (2πfC)","Capacitive reactance"],["XL = 2πfL","Inductive reactance"],["dB = 20 · log₁₀(V₂/V₁)","Voltage ratio in decibels"],["dB = 10 · log₁₀(P₂/P₁)","Power ratio in decibels"],["f₀ = 1 / (2π√(LC))","LC resonant frequency"]],"labs":["ac","db"],"quiz":[{"q":"A sine wave peaks at 10 V. Its RMS value is about:","o":["5 V","7.07 V","10 V","14.1 V"],"a":1,"w":"10 / √2 ≈ 7.07 V."},{"q":"An amplifier boosts a 10 mV signal to 1 V. Its gain is:","o":["20 dB","40 dB","100 dB","10 dB"],"a":1,"w":"The ratio is 100, and 20 × log₁₀(100) = 40 dB."},{"q":"+3 dB of power gain multiplies power by about:","o":["1.4","2","3","10"],"a":1,"w":"10^(3/10) ≈ 2."},{"q":"As frequency goes up, a capacitor's reactance:","o":["Goes up","Goes down","Stays the same","Becomes negative resistance"],"a":1,"w":"Xc = 1 / (2πfC), so higher frequency gives lower reactance. At high frequencies a capacitor looks almost like a short."},{"q":"What is the period of a 50 Hz mains waveform?","o":["50 ms","20 ms","5 ms","2 ms"],"a":1,"w":"T = 1 / 50 = 0.02 s = 20 ms."}],"reading":"pp. 80–94, 155–223","part":"C"},
{"n":15,"title":"Op Amps","short":"Op Amps","blurb":"Two golden rules, the classic amplifier circuits, and comparators.","big":"An op amp is an amplifier with enormous gain. On its own it's almost unusable, but wrapped in negative feedback it becomes precise and predictable. Two rules let you analyze nearly any op amp circuit in seconds. Run it without feedback and it becomes a comparator, and adding a little positive feedback gives the comparator hysteresis so noise can't make it flicker.","ideas":["Golden rule 1 (with negative feedback): the output does whatever it takes to make V+ equal V−.","Golden rule 2: the inputs draw essentially no current.","Non-inverting amplifier gain = 1 + Rf/Rin. Inverting amplifier gain = −Rf/Rin.","A voltage follower (gain 1) buffers a weak source so the next stage can't load it down.","The output cannot swing beyond the supply rails, and many op amps can't get near them. Rail-to-rail parts get within millivolts.","Without feedback, an op amp is a comparator. Its output jumps to one rail or the other.","Hysteresis creates two thresholds, so a noisy signal crossing the threshold switches the output only once.","For comparing voltages, a dedicated comparator chip is faster and cleaner than an op amp."],"formulas":[["G = 1 + Rf / Rin","Non-inverting gain"],["G = −Rf / Rin","Inverting gain"],["Vout = −Rf · (V₁/R₁ + V₂/R₂)","Summing amplifier"],["Vout = Vin","Voltage follower"]],"labs":["opamp","hyst"],"quiz":[{"q":"A non-inverting amplifier has Rf = 9 kΩ and Rin = 1 kΩ. Its gain is:","o":["9","10","−9","0.1"],"a":1,"w":"G = 1 + 9k/1k = 10."},{"q":"An inverting amplifier with gain −5 gets +1.2 V on ±9 V rails. The output is about:","o":["+6 V","−6 V","−9 V","0 V"],"a":1,"w":"−5 × 1.2 = −6 V, which is still inside the rails."},{"q":"Why add hysteresis to a comparator?","o":["To increase gain","To stop the output toggling rapidly when a noisy input sits near the threshold","To reduce power","To make it linear"],"a":1,"w":"Positive feedback creates two thresholds, so small noise can't flip the output back and forth."},{"q":"What is a voltage follower for?","o":["Doubling a voltage","Buffering, so a high-impedance source can drive a load","Making a square wave","Rectifying AC"],"a":1,"w":"Its gain is 1, but it has a very high input impedance and a low output impedance."},{"q":"In an inverting amplifier, the inverting input sits at about:","o":["The input voltage","0 V (virtual ground)","The positive rail","Half the output"],"a":1,"w":"The non-inverting input is grounded, and feedback forces V− to match it."}],"reading":"pp. 635–662","part":"C"},
{"n":16,"title":"Filters","short":"Filters","blurb":"Low-pass, high-pass and band-pass filters, passive and active.","big":"Filters pass some frequencies and reject others. The simplest is one resistor plus one capacitor, with a cutoff where the output has fallen to about 71%, or −3 dB. Beyond the cutoff, a first-order filter rolls off at 20 dB per decade. Active filters add an op amp for steeper slopes and gain without loading the stage before.","ideas":["Cutoff frequency for a single RC stage: fc = 1/(2πRC).","At the cutoff frequency the output is −3 dB, which is 70.7% of the input voltage.","First-order filters roll off at −20 dB per decade. Each extra order adds another −20 dB per decade.","Low-pass keeps the slow parts of a signal. High-pass keeps the fast parts and blocks DC.","Filters also shift phase. A first-order low-pass is 45° behind at its cutoff.","Butterworth filters are flattest in the passband, Chebyshev filters are steeper but ripple, and Bessel filters preserve the shape of a waveform.","An RC low-pass turns PWM into a smooth voltage and cleans noise off a sensor line before an ADC."],"formulas":[["fc = 1 / (2πRC)","RC cutoff frequency"],["|H| = 1 / √(1 + (f/fc)²)","RC low-pass gain"],["|H| = (f/fc) / √(1 + (f/fc)²)","RC high-pass gain"],["φ = −arctan(f/fc)","Low-pass phase shift"]],"labs":["filter"],"quiz":[{"q":"R = 1.6 kΩ and C = 100 nF. The cutoff frequency is about:","o":["100 Hz","1 kHz","10 kHz","16 kHz"],"a":1,"w":"fc = 1 / (2π × 1600 × 100 nF) ≈ 995 Hz."},{"q":"A first-order low-pass filter attenuates a signal at 10× the cutoff frequency by about:","o":["−3 dB","−6 dB","−20 dB","−40 dB"],"a":2,"w":"One decade above cutoff, it's down about 20 dB, which is one tenth of the voltage."},{"q":"At the cutoff frequency, the output voltage is:","o":["50% of the input","70.7% of the input","100% of the input","10% of the input"],"a":1,"w":"−3 dB in voltage is 1/√2 ≈ 0.707."},{"q":"Which filter would remove a DC offset from an audio signal?","o":["Low-pass","High-pass","Neither","Both in series"],"a":1,"w":"DC is 0 Hz, the lowest frequency of all, so a high-pass filter blocks it."},{"q":"A second-order low-pass rolls off at:","o":["−10 dB/decade","−20 dB/decade","−40 dB/decade","−60 dB/decade"],"a":2,"w":"Each order adds −20 dB per decade."}],"reading":"pp. 663–682","part":"C"},
{"n":17,"title":"Sound and Audio","short":"Audio","blurb":"How sound works, microphones, amplifiers, speakers and crossovers.","big":"Audio pulls together op amps, filters, decibels and impedance. Sound is pressure waves between about 20 Hz and 20 kHz. Microphones turn those waves into millivolt signals, preamps boost them, power amplifiers drive speakers, and crossovers split the sound so each driver handles the frequencies it's good at.","ideas":["Human hearing spans roughly 20 Hz to 20 kHz, and the top end drops with age.","The speed of sound in air is about 343 m/s, so wavelength = 343 / f.","Electret microphones need a small bias voltage and output millivolt signals, so they need a preamplifier.","Class-AB amplifiers are linear and simple but run warm. Class-D amplifiers switch at high frequency and are very efficient.","Speakers are rated by impedance, commonly 4 Ω or 8 Ω. The amplifier must be able to drive that load.","Square and sawtooth waves sound harsher than sine waves because they contain harmonics, which are multiples of the base frequency.","A crossover sends low frequencies to the woofer and high frequencies to the tweeter."],"formulas":[["λ = v / f","Wavelength (v ≈ 343 m/s in air)"],["P = Vrms² / R","Speaker power"],["f_n = n · f₀","Harmonics"]],"labs":["tone"],"quiz":[{"q":"An 8 Ω speaker driven at 4 V RMS receives:","o":["0.5 W","2 W","4 W","32 W"],"a":1,"w":"P = V² / R = 16 / 8 = 2 W."},{"q":"The wavelength of a 343 Hz tone in air is about:","o":["1 cm","1 m","10 m","343 m"],"a":1,"w":"λ = 343 / 343 = 1 m."},{"q":"Why does a square wave sound buzzier than a sine wave at the same pitch?","o":["It's louder","It contains many odd harmonics","It has a lower frequency","It has no harmonics"],"a":1,"w":"A square wave is a sine plus odd harmonics at 3×, 5×, 7× and so on. Those extra frequencies add the buzz."},{"q":"Which amplifier class is the most efficient?","o":["Class A","Class AB","Class D","They are all equal"],"a":2,"w":"Class-D switches its output transistors fully on or off, so they waste very little power."},{"q":"In a two-way speaker, the tweeter gets frequencies through a:","o":["Low-pass filter","High-pass filter","Notch filter","Voltage divider"],"a":1,"w":"The tweeter handles high frequencies, so it sits behind a high-pass section of the crossover."}],"reading":"pp. 947–962","part":"C"}
];
const PARTS={A:{name:"Foundations",desc:"Theory and bench skills that everything else depends on."},B:{name:"Building",desc:"The parts and circuits that show up in real projects."},C:{name:"Going deeper",desc:"Signals, amplifiers and filters, for designing your own circuits."}};
const BOOK={title:"Practical Electronics for Inventors",ed:"4th edition",by:"Paul Scherz and Simon Monk"};
window.SB_DATA={M,PARTS,BOOK};
})();
